window.EVA_GRAMMAR_EXPANSIONS={"103":{"intro":"State verbs describe thoughts, possession, emotions, senses and states. Many are normally simple rather than continuous, though several verbs change meaning in continuous forms.","sections":[{"title":"State and action meanings","explain":"Do not apply a blanket ban on continuous forms. Ask whether the verb names a stable state or an active process.","points":["I know the answer (state); not usually 'I am knowing'.","I have a car (possession); I'm having lunch (activity).","I think this is right (opinion); I'm thinking about it (mental process).","It smells nice (perception); She is smelling the flowers (deliberate action).","I see your point (understand); I'm seeing a doctor tomorrow (arrangement)."],"examples":[["I believe this is correct.","Belief as a state."],["She is having a break.","Have as an activity."],["He is thinking about a new job.","An active process."]]},{"title":"Continuous for temporary behaviour and change","explain":"Some stative-looking expressions appear in progressive forms to emphasise temporary behaviour or a developing situation.","points":["You're being rude (temporary behaviour), versus You are rude (general judgement).","I'm loving this course is possible as an informal emphatic statement, despite traditional teaching rules.","The situation is improving (a developing change).","Choose standard neutral forms for beginner writing unless the nuance is intentional."],"examples":[["You're being unusually quiet today.","Temporary behaviour."],["I understand the instructions.","Usual state form."]]}],"mistakes":[["I am knowing him.","I know him.","Know is normally stative."],["I am having a house.","I have a house.","Possession uses simple form."],["He thinks about the answer right now (emphasis on process).","He is thinking about the answer right now.","Continuous highlights activity."]],"quiz":[["Possession:",["I am having a car.","I have a car.","I has a car."],1,"Have possession usually simple."],["Active thought process:",["I'm thinking about it.","I think about it now only.","I'm knowing about it."],0,"Thinking shows process."],["'You're being rude' suggests:",["temporary behaviour","permanent truth only","past event"],0,"Being + adjective can describe current behaviour."]],"related":["034","043","045","137"]},"109":{"intro":"The second conditional discusses imagined, unlikely or hypothetical situations in the present or future. It commonly uses if + past form, would + base verb.","sections":[{"title":"Unreal present and future","explain":"A past verb form after if does not necessarily refer to past time; it can mark distance from reality.","points":["If I had more time, I would study every day.","If she knew the answer, she would tell us.","Could/might can replace would in the result: I could help.","With be, were is traditional and useful in formal hypotheticals: If I were you, I'd wait.","Never combine would with the normal if-clause in a basic second conditional."],"examples":[["If I lived nearer, I would cycle.","Imagined current situation."],["If I were you, I would ask for advice.","Hypothetical advice."],["If we had a bigger budget, we could expand.","Imagined possibility."]]},{"title":"Contrast with first conditional","explain":"First conditional suggests a realistic event; second conditional imagines a situation or presents it as distant.","points":["If it rains tomorrow, we'll stay in (real possibility).","If it rained every day, we'd need another plan (hypothetical pattern).","If you see Maya, tell her (possible); if you saw Maya, what would you say? (imagined)."],"examples":[["If I win the prize, I'll celebrate.","Real future possibility."],["If I won the prize, I'd travel.","Imagined scenario."]]}],"mistakes":[["If I would know, I would help.","If I knew, I would help.","Past form in if-clause, would in main clause."],["If I was you, I will wait (formal hypothetical).","If I were you, I would wait.","Were + would is a conventional formal form."],["If she had time, she will help (hypothetical).","If she had time, she would help.","Would suits unreal condition."]],"quiz":[["Complete: If I ____ rich, I would travel.",["am","were","will be"],1,"Were marks a hypothetical condition."],["Second conditional usually expresses:",["unreal present/future","certain past fact","routine truth"],0,"Imagined situation."],["Choose the main clause.",["I would help.","I will helped.","I helping."],0,"Would + base verb."]],"related":["086","088","148","149"]},"111":{"intro":"Conditional connectors add important limits to promises and plans. Unless, as long as and provided that do not all mean the same thing.","sections":[{"title":"Unless = except if","explain":"Unless introduces an exception to what would normally happen. It often replaces if ... not, but the meanings do not align in every complex sentence, so check the logic.","points":["We will leave unless it rains = we will leave except if it rains.","Do not add another negative casually: Unless you don't want to... may reverse the meaning.","Unless usually refers to an exception rather than simply asking whether something happens."],"examples":[["You cannot enter unless you have a pass.","Without a pass, entry is not permitted."],["Unless there is a delay, we will arrive at six.","A delay is the only stated exception."]]},{"title":"As long as, provided that and in case","explain":"As long as and provided that state conditions; provided that is somewhat more formal. In case signals a precaution against a possible event and is not interchangeable with if.","points":["You may use the room as long as you leave it clean.","The offer is valid provided that you apply by Friday.","Take an umbrella in case it rains = precaution now for possible rain.","If it rains, we will stay inside = action depends on actual rain."],"examples":[["You can borrow the book provided that you return it.","Formal condition."],["I saved a copy in case the file was lost.","Precaution."]]}],"mistakes":[["Unless you do not hurry, you will miss it.","Unless you hurry, you will miss it.","The unnecessary negative changes the logic."],["Take a coat if it gets cold (for a precaution now).","Take a coat in case it gets cold.","In case gives the precaution meaning."],["I will go provided you will help.","I will go provided that you help.","Use present simple for a real future condition."]],"quiz":[["'Unless it rains' means:",["only if it rains","except if it rains","because it rains"],1,"Unless = except if."],["Choose a precaution.",["Take water in case you're thirsty.","Take water provided you are thirsty.","Take water unless you're thirsty."],0,"In case expresses preparation."],["Formal condition connector:",["provided that","and","yet"],0,"Provided that sets a condition."]],"related":["086","088","091","169"]},"113":{"intro":"Defining relative clauses identify exactly which person, thing, place or time you mean. Leaving them out can change the reference.","sections":[{"title":"Precise defining clauses","explain":"A defining clause is essential to identifying the intended noun. Modern British English allows who/that for people and which/that for things, with stylistic choices.","points":["the applicants who submitted the form (identifies applicants).","the information that we requested (identifies information).","whose + noun expresses possession: the person whose car was stolen.","where refers to place: the office where I work.","when refers to time: the day when we met.","No commas around essential defining clauses."],"examples":[["The employee whose badge was lost reported it.","Whose shows possession."],["The room where we met is upstairs.","Where refers to a location."],["The forms that arrived yesterday are complete.","Identifies forms."]]},{"title":"Omission and prepositions","explain":"An object relative pronoun may be left out. A relative preposition can remain at the end of a natural clause or be moved formally to the front.","points":["the person (who/that) I phoned = object omitted.","the person who phoned me = subject, not omitted.","the colleague I spoke to = natural; the colleague to whom I spoke = formal.","Avoid doubling the relative object: 'the person who I called her'."],"examples":[["The topic we talked about is important.","Object relative omitted, preposition at end."],["This is the policy on which we agreed.","Formal preposition-fronting."]]}],"mistakes":[["The woman who she helped me","The woman who helped me","Remove duplicated subject."],["The letter which I posted it","The letter which I posted","Remove repeated object."],["The clinic, where I work, is open (when identifying one clinic among many).","The clinic where I work is open.","Use no commas for an essential identifying clause."]],"quiz":[["In a defining clause, commas are usually:",["absent","mandatory","placed after every noun"],0,"No commas around restrictive information."],["Choose the correct phrase.",["the colleague whose car broke down","the colleague which car broke down","the colleague who car broke down"],0,"Whose signals possession."],["Which object relative is natural?",["the report I wrote","the report I wrote it","the report wrote me"],0,"Object relative may be omitted."]],"related":["090","114","130","159"]},"114":{"intro":"Non-defining relative clauses give additional information about a noun that is already identifiable. They use commas and do not normally use that.","sections":[{"title":"Additional information and commas","explain":"A non-defining clause is parenthetical. Remove it, and the main reference still makes sense. In writing, place a comma before and after it if the sentence continues.","points":["My sister, who lives in Bristol, is visiting.","London, which is the capital of England, has many museums.","Do not use that for non-defining relative clauses.","The relative pronoun is not usually omitted in a non-defining clause.","Whose can appear: My colleague, whose car broke down, took the bus."],"examples":[["Mr Patel, who teaches English, has arrived.","Extra information about Mr Patel."],["The package, which arrived yesterday, is damaged.","Which adds non-essential information."],["Our manager, whose office is upstairs, is away.","Non-defining possession."]]},{"title":"Meaning changes with punctuation","explain":"Commas can change whether a clause selects a subgroup or simply describes everyone/the whole group.","points":["The employees who completed the training received certificates = only the completers.","The employees, who completed the training, received certificates = extra information suggesting all relevant employees completed it.","Which can refer to a whole previous clause: He forgot the meeting, which surprised everyone."],"examples":[["The students who studied passed.","Identifies a subset."],["The students, who had all studied, passed.","Adds extra information about an identified group."]]}],"mistakes":[["My car, that is old, broke down.","My car, which is old, broke down.","That is not used in non-defining relatives."],["London which is large is busy.","London, which is large, is busy.","Add commas for extra information."],["My friend, I met last year, is here.","My friend, whom I met last year, is here.","Do not omit object relative in non-defining clauses."]],"quiz":[["Choose the non-defining clause.",["My mum, who lives in Bath, is visiting.","the person that helped us","the book I bought"],0,"Commas mark extra information."],["Can non-defining clauses normally use that?",["yes, always","no","only for people"],1,"Use who/which/whose, not that."],["Commas can change:",["which people are identified","only font size","nothing"],0,"Restrictive versus parenthetical meaning differs."]],"related":["090","113","130","159"]},"115":{"intro":"Reported speech tells somebody what another person said without repeating the exact words. Changes of pronoun, tense, time and place depend on the reporting situation.","sections":[{"title":"Direct speech and reported statements","explain":"Direct speech uses the speaker's exact words, often with quotation marks. Reported speech describes the message from another viewpoint.","points":["Direct: Sara said, 'I am tired.' Reported: Sara said (that) she was tired.","Say normally has no person as a direct object: said that... / said to me. Tell normally needs a person: told me that...","That may be omitted in many statements: He said he was ready.","Choose pronouns to match who is reporting: my, your, our and I may all change."],"examples":[["'I work here,' she said. → She said that she worked there.","Present simple normally becomes past simple after past reporting."],["'We have finished,' they said. → They said they had finished.","Present perfect often becomes past perfect."],["The manager told us that the meeting was cancelled.","Tell + object + that-clause."]]},{"title":"Backshift is common, not automatic","explain":"When the reporting verb is in the past, many tenses move one step back. If the information remains true and relevant, a speaker may keep the original tense.","points":["am/is/are → was/were; work → worked; am working → was working.","have done → had done; did → had done; will → would; can → could.","Past perfect normally stays past perfect.","A continuing fact can remain present: The teacher said water boils at 100°C (under standard pressure).","Always consider whether now, today, tomorrow and here still make sense from the reporting point."],"examples":[["He said he would call the next day.","Will → would and tomorrow → the next day, when viewpoint changes."],["She said the office opens at nine.","Present retained because the timetable is still current."]]}],"mistakes":[["She said me that she was busy.","She told me that she was busy.","Tell needs an object; say does not take it directly."],["He told that he had left.","He said that he had left.","Tell generally requires a person object."],["He said he is arriving yesterday.","He said he had arrived the previous day.","Match tense and time reference to the reporting context."]],"quiz":[["Report: 'I am late,' Tom said.",["Tom said that he was late.","Tom told that he is late.","Tom said me he was late."],0,"Am normally backshifts to was."],["Choose the natural verb pattern.",["She said me the news.","She told me the news.","She told to me the news."],1,"Tell takes a person object."],["If a fact is still true, the original tense:",["must always backshift","may sometimes be retained","must become future"],1,"Backshift is not compulsory for an unchanged current fact."]],"related":["116","117","156","157"]},"116":{"intro":"A reported question uses normal statement word order. It is not written with the original auxiliary inversion, and a question mark is not normally used after a reporting statement.","sections":[{"title":"Yes/no reported questions","explain":"Use asked (someone) if or whether + subject + verb. The embedded clause uses statement order.","points":["Direct: 'Are you ready?' → She asked if I was ready.","Direct: 'Did he call?' → She asked whether he had called.","Do/does/did used only for asking the direct question often disappears in the reported clause.","If and whether both work in many yes/no reports; whether is common in more formal contexts and before or not."],"examples":[["'Do you work on Fridays?' → He asked whether I worked on Fridays.","No did/do inversion in the reported clause."],["'Has the parcel arrived?' → She asked if the parcel had arrived.","Subject precedes had."],["She asked me whether I wanted a receipt.","Ask + object + whether-clause."]]},{"title":"Wh- reported questions","explain":"Keep what, when, where, why, how, who or which, but change to statement word order. Adjust pronouns and time when appropriate.","points":["Where do you live? → He asked where I lived.","What are you doing? → She asked what I was doing.","Why did they leave? → He asked why they had left.","The reporting sentence normally ends with a full stop, not a question mark.","A subject-question may not show inversion even directly: Who called? → She asked who had called."],"examples":[["'Where is the station?' → He asked where the station was.","Use station was, not was the station."],["'When will it start?' → She asked when it would start.","Will often backshifts to would."]]}],"mistakes":[["He asked where was I.","He asked where I was.","Reported questions use subject–verb order."],["She asked did I agree.","She asked if I agreed.","Use if/whether, no direct did."],["He asked why did they leave.","He asked why they had left.","No question inversion; tense backshift is common."]],"quiz":[["Report 'Where are you going?'",["She asked where was I going.","She asked where I was going.","She asked where did I go."],1,"Embedded clause has statement order."],["Report 'Did you call?'",["He asked if I had called.","He asked did I called.","He asked if had I called."],0,"If + subject + had + participle."],["A reported yes/no question commonly starts with:",["if or whether","because","although"],0,"If/whether introduces an embedded yes/no question."]],"related":["115","117","132","156"]},"117":{"intro":"Commands and requests are usually reported with a reporting verb + person + (not) to-infinitive. Choose told, asked, advised, warned or reminded for the speaker's intention.","sections":[{"title":"Report commands and requests","explain":"An imperative has no expressed subject. The usual reported form does not need a tense shift because it uses to + verb.","points":["'Close the window.' → She told me to close the window.","'Please wait.' → He asked us to wait.","'Don't touch that.' → She warned us not to touch it.","Told and asked normally include the person receiving the command/request.","Do not report ordinary imperatives as 'said me to'."],"examples":[["The receptionist asked us to sign the form.","Polite request."],["The supervisor told them not to enter.","Negative instruction."],["The teacher reminded us to bring our books.","Reminder."]]},{"title":"Advice, suggestions and reporting verbs","explain":"Some verbs use an infinitive, whereas others use -ing or that-clauses. A suggestion is not always a command.","points":["advise someone to do; warn someone not to do; invite someone to do.","suggest doing something; suggest that someone (should) do something.","Recommend doing; recommend that ... (should) ...","In British English, 'suggest me to do' is not standard.","Choose the reporting verb based on tone: order is stronger than ask."],"examples":[["She suggested taking a short break.","Suggest + -ing."],["He advised me to check the details.","Advise + object + to-infinitive."]]}],"mistakes":[["She said me to wait.","She told me to wait.","Use told + person + to verb."],["He told me don't go.","He told me not to go.","Not comes before the infinitive."],["She suggested me to apply.","She suggested applying.","Suggest does not normally use object + to-infinitive."]],"quiz":[["Report: 'Don't move,' he said to me.",["He told me not to move.","He told me don't move.","He said me not move."],0,"Told + person + not to."],["Choose a correct suggestion.",["She suggested to leave.","She suggested leaving.","She suggested me to leave."],1,"Suggest + -ing."],["Which verb fits an instruction to avoid danger?",["warned","entertained","complimented"],0,"Warned reports a warning."]],"related":["115","116","156","157"]},"118":{"intro":"The tense of a passive is carried by the auxiliary be, while the main verb stays in the past participle. Here we focus on past and future passive forms.","sections":[{"title":"Past simple passive","explain":"Use was/were + past participle for completed past events where the receiver of the action is the topic.","points":["Singular: The report was published in 2021.","Plural: The results were announced yesterday.","Negative: The forms were not accepted.","Question: Was the parcel delivered?","Irregular participles matter: built, sent, written, taken, given."],"examples":[["The bridge was built in the nineteenth century.","Was + built."],["The letters were sent yesterday.","Were + sent."],["Was the decision explained clearly?","Past passive question."]]},{"title":"Future passive","explain":"Use will be + past participle for future predictions, decisions and announcements; use be going to be + participle for an intended or expected plan.","points":["The results will be released tomorrow.","The building is going to be renovated next year.","Negative: The service will not be interrupted.","Question: Will the documents be checked?","Keep the main past participle constant whatever the future auxiliary is."],"examples":[["The new course will be introduced next month.","Will be + introduced."],["The windows are going to be replaced.","Planned future passive."]]}],"mistakes":[["The email was send.","The email was sent.","Sent is the past participle."],["The forms will checked.","The forms will be checked.","Future passive needs be."],["The report were prepared.","The report was prepared.","Singular report takes was."]],"quiz":[["Past passive of 'They repaired the road':",["The road was repaired.","The road repaired.","The road has repair."],0,"Was + past participle."],["Future passive needs:",["will + participle","will be + participle","will been + participle"],1,"Will be repaired."],["Plural past passive starts with:",["were","was","is"],0,"Were for plural subjects."]],"related":["094","119","153","200"]},"119":{"intro":"Modal passives express rules, advice, possibility, permission or necessity without naming the person who performs the action.","sections":[{"title":"Modal + be + past participle","explain":"After a modal, use the bare infinitive be, followed by the past participle. The form stays the same for singular and plural subjects.","points":["must be checked (obligation); must not be shared (prohibition).","should be reviewed (advice); can be reused (ability/possibility).","may be delayed; might be changed; could be improved.","The subject controls meaning, but the modal itself does not gain -s."],"examples":[["The equipment must be inspected.","Obligation in a passive."],["These results should be checked again.","Advice without identifying the checker."],["Your appointment may be delayed.","Possibility in the passive."]]},{"title":"Modal-perfect passive","explain":"To express judgments about earlier events, advanced English uses modal + have been + past participle.","points":["The email might have been sent yesterday (uncertain past possibility).","The report should have been submitted (past expectation; perhaps it was not).","The documents must have been misplaced (strong deduction about the past).","Do not omit been in a modal-perfect passive."],"examples":[["The door could have been left unlocked.","Past possibility."],["The error should have been noticed sooner.","Past criticism or unrealised expectation."]]}],"mistakes":[["must checked","must be checked","After must, use be + participle."],["should be check","should be checked","Use the past participle checked."],["might have sent by them","might have been sent by them","Modal-perfect passive needs have been."]],"quiz":[["Choose the modal passive.",["must inspected","must be inspected","must being inspect"],1,"Modal + be + participle."],["Past modal passive:",["could have been delayed","could be have delayed","could been delay"],0,"Could have been + participle."],["'Should be checked' expresses:",["advice/expectation","completed past certainty","a direct question"],0,"Should recommends action."]],"related":["094","118","153","200"]},"121":{"intro":"Many nouns are countable in one meaning but uncountable in another. Professional writing becomes clearer when the noun's intended meaning drives the determiner.","sections":[{"title":"Meaning changes countability","explain":"Do not memorise a word as permanently countable or uncountable without checking the meaning in context.","points":["experience = general knowledge (mass); an experience = one event (count).","chicken = meat (mass); a chicken = an animal (count).","paper = material (mass); a paper = an academic article (count).","room = space (mass); a room = part of a building (count).","business = commercial activity (mass); a business = company (count)."],"examples":[["She has experience in oncology.","Mass: expertise."],["It was a difficult experience.","Count: event."],["We need more room.","Mass: space."]]},{"title":"Common professional mass nouns","explain":"A number of high-frequency English words that are plural in other languages are normally mass nouns in English.","points":["information, advice, furniture, equipment, luggage, homework, research, progress, accommodation (often mass in BrE).","a piece of advice; two pieces of equipment; an item of information.","Research can refer to a body of work without plural -s; specific studies are countable studies.","Some specialist or dialectal uses exist, but standard formal English prefers these mass patterns."],"examples":[["This research provides useful evidence.","Mass noun."],["We need three items of equipment.","Unit counter."]]}],"mistakes":[["several researches (general studies)","several studies","Use studies for separate research projects."],["an equipment","a piece of equipment","Equipment is generally mass."],["many accommodations (BrE lodging in general)","plenty of accommodation","Accommodation is often mass in British English."]],"quiz":[["Choose the countable event sense.",["an experience","an advice","an equipment"],0,"Experience can count separate events."],["Choose a mass form.",["useful research","two researches","a research"],0,"Research is usually mass."],["Choose the BrE unit expression.",["two equipments","two pieces of equipment","two equipment"],1,"Piece counts equipment."]],"related":["009","025","026","123"]},"123":{"intro":"Articles depend on whether a noun refers to something generally, specifically, uniquely, or as a representative of a class.","sections":[{"title":"Identifiability rather than simple mention","explain":"The is appropriate even on first mention if the situation clearly identifies the referent. A/an may reintroduce a member of a category.","points":["Please close the door (the door is obvious).","I spoke to a manager (not yet identified).","The manager of our team called (identified by phrase).","A tiger is a large cat / Tigers are large cats / The tiger is a large cat (different ways to generalise; the last is more formal/class-level).","Specific plurals/mass nouns can take the; general ones often do not."],"examples":[["The roof needs repairing.","Unique roof in the current building context."],["We need an engineer.","One non-specified engineer."],["The engineers working here are experienced.","A defined group."]]},{"title":"Institutions, meals, jobs and superlatives","explain":"Article choice can depend on a fixed expression or the role in which someone visits a place.","points":["go to school (attend as pupil) versus go to the school (visit the particular building).","go to bed; at work; by car; after lunch (common zero-article patterns).","She is a nurse (a/an with singular profession).","the best option; the first attempt (usual superlative/ordinal pattern).","the information you sent (specific mass noun)."],"examples":[["We visited the school yesterday.","Building visited rather than 'attend school'."],["He is an engineer.","Profession with an."]]}],"mistakes":[["She is nurse.","She is a nurse.","Singular count profession normally takes article."],["I need the advice (no specific advice identified).","I need advice.","General mass noun often has zero article."],["the breakfast at seven (ordinary routine).","breakfast at seven","Routine meal expressions often use zero article."]],"quiz":[["Complete: She is ____ architect.",["a","an","—"],1,"Architect begins with a vowel sound."],["Which is general?",["Education is important.","The education I got was important.","This education is important."],0,"Uncountable abstract general statement."],["'The door' on first mention may be correct when:",["the referent is obvious","all doors are unknown","there are infinitely many doors"],0,"Shared situational knowledge."]],"related":["027","067","161","219"]},"124":{"intro":"The choice between -ing and to-infinitive is determined by the first verb, the meaning and sometimes the situation. It is not a simple past-versus-future rule.","sections":[{"title":"Recognise fixed patterns","explain":"Most common patterns must be memorised in chunks. A little practice with real sentences is better than a long isolated verb list.","points":["avoid, consider, deny, enjoy, finish, imagine, mind, risk, suggest + -ing.","afford, agree, decide, expect, hope, manage, offer, refuse, seem, want + to-infinitive.","continue, begin, start, like, love, prefer may take either in many contexts.","Preposition + -ing: insist on paying; look forward to meeting.","Adjective + to-infinitive: happy to help, ready to go."],"examples":[["They managed to solve the issue.","Manage + infinitive."],["She avoided making an assumption.","Avoid + -ing."],["He refused to sign the document.","Refuse + infinitive."]]},{"title":"When changing the form changes meaning","explain":"Some verbs change the time relationship or intended result of the second action. This is often tested in intermediate English.","points":["remember to phone = do not forget a future duty; remember phoning = recall a past action.","forget to lock = fail to lock; forget locking = not remember having locked.","stop to speak = pause in order to speak; stop speaking = cease speaking.","try to open = make an effort; try opening = test a method.","regret to inform = formal announcement; regret telling = feel sorry about a past action."],"examples":[["I remember sending the form.","I recall doing it."],["Please remember to send the form.","Do not forget the future task."]]}],"mistakes":[["suggested to wait","suggested waiting","Suggest usually takes -ing or a that-clause."],["managed finishing","managed to finish","Manage + to-infinitive."],["remember locking tomorrow","remember to lock it tomorrow","For a future duty, use remember to."]],"quiz":[["Complete: We managed ____ on time.",["arriving","to arrive","arrive"],1,"Manage + to-infinitive."],["'I remember meeting her' means:",["I recall the meeting.","I have to meet her.","I will meet her tomorrow."],0,"-ing refers to the remembered event."],["Choose a standard pattern.",["avoid to speak","avoid speaking","avoid speak"],1,"Avoid + -ing."]],"related":["089","166","198"]},"126":{"intro":"Some adjectives are followed by particular prepositions. Learn the combination with a whole example, because direct translation often gives the wrong preposition.","sections":[{"title":"Common adjective + preposition patterns","explain":"The following combinations are frequent in British English. A different preposition may change the meaning.","points":["afraid of; proud of; fond of; aware of; capable of","good at; bad at; surprised at/by; excellent at","interested in; involved in; successful in","responsible for; famous for; ready for; suitable for","kind to; married to; similar to; different from (different to is also common in UK speech)"],"examples":[["She is responsible for the timetable.","Responsible takes for."],["We are interested in the course.","Interested takes in."],["He is very good at explaining procedures.","Good at + -ing activity."]]},{"title":"What follows a preposition?","explain":"A preposition can be followed by a noun, pronoun or -ing form, not a plain infinitive. Some adjectives take different prepositions with different meanings.","points":["I am excited about starting, not excited about start.","angry with a person; angry about a situation.","sorry for a mistake; sorry about a problem; sorry to hear bad news."],"examples":[["I am worried about missing the train.","About + -ing."],["She was angry with the supplier about the delay.","Different prepositions have different roles."]]}],"mistakes":[["interested on music","interested in music","Use interested in."],["good in maths","good at maths","Good at is the usual skill pattern."],["responsible of staff","responsible for staff","Responsible for is standard."]],"quiz":[["She is afraid ____ spiders.",["from","of","with"],1,"Afraid of."],["The team is good ____ solving problems.",["at","in","for"],0,"Good at + -ing."],["Complete: responsible ____ training.",["of","with","for"],2,"Responsible for."]],"related":["030","167","198"]},"127":{"intro":"A phrasal verb combines a verb with an adverbial or prepositional particle. The meaning is often more specific than the individual words suggest.","sections":[{"title":"Common get, give, take and put combinations","explain":"Learn phrasal verbs in real sentences. One combination may have several meanings depending on context.","points":["get up = leave bed; get on with = have a good relationship/continue work; get over = recover.","give up = stop trying/a habit; give away = donate or reveal; give back = return.","take off = remove or depart (aircraft); take over = assume control; take up = begin a hobby.","put on = wear; put off = postpone; put away = return to storage.","A literal meaning does not always predict the figurative one."],"examples":[["She gave up smoking last year.","Stop a habit."],["The flight took off at eight.","Aircraft departure."],["We put off the meeting until Friday.","Postpone."]]},{"title":"Objects and word order","explain":"Some phrasal verbs are separable; others take a preposition and keep the object afterwards. Pronouns are especially important.","points":["turn off the light / turn the light off / turn it off (not turn off it).","look after the child / look after her (not look her after).","give up smoking and give it up.","Some phrases are intransitive: The car broke down.","Learn whether each verb is transitive, intransitive, separable or inseparable."],"examples":[["Please turn it down.","Pronoun between turn and down."],["She looked after her neighbour.","Look after stays together."]]}],"mistakes":[["turn off it","turn it off","Separable particle verb with pronoun."],["look her after","look after her","Prepositional verb is inseparable."],["The car broke down it.","The car broke down.","Break down here has no object."]],"quiz":[["Correct pronoun order:",["turn off it","turn it off","turn it"],1,"Pronoun before particle."],["'Put off' can mean:",["postpone","put clothing on","finish"],0,"Put off a meeting = postpone."],["Correct pattern:",["look it after","look after it","look after"],1,"Look after + object."]],"related":["168","209","222"]},"129":{"intro":"Linking words help a reader follow your argument. However, therefore, although and because do different grammatical jobs, even when they seem similar in meaning.","sections":[{"title":"Conjunction or linking adverb?","explain":"Although and because introduce dependent clauses. However and therefore are conjunctive adverbs or discourse connectors; they do not normally join two independent clauses with just a comma.","points":["Although it was expensive, we bought it.","It was expensive. However, we bought it.","It was expensive; however, we bought it.","We needed it. Therefore, we bought it.","Avoid a comma splice: 'It was expensive, however, we bought it' is not the standard punctuation for two complete clauses."],"examples":[["The evidence was limited; therefore, we waited.","Semicolon joins complete clauses with a connector."],["Although the evidence was limited, we acted.","Although creates a dependent clause."],["We waited because the evidence was limited.","Because adds a reason."]]},{"title":"Contrast, result and addition","explain":"Use connectors to make logical relationships explicit. Do not add an impressive connector when it does not fit the meaning.","points":["Contrast/concession: but, although, even though, however, nevertheless.","Result: so, therefore, consequently, as a result.","Addition: and, also, furthermore, in addition.","Example: for example, for instance, such as.","Nevertheless is often more formal than but."],"examples":[["The costs increased. Nevertheless, demand remained strong.","Concession in formal writing."],["The data were incomplete. As a result, the decision was delayed.","Clear consequence."]]}],"mistakes":[["The meeting ended, however, we stayed.","The meeting ended; however, we stayed.","Two independent clauses need correct joining punctuation."],["Although he practised. He was nervous.","Although he practised, he was nervous.","A dependent clause cannot normally stand alone here."],["The cause was unclear. For example, we stopped.","The cause was unclear. Therefore, we stopped.","Therefore fits a result; for example introduces an instance."]],"quiz":[["Choose the correct sentence.",["It was late, however, we worked.","It was late; however, we worked.","Although it was late. We worked."],1,"Use a semicolon or full stop before however joining independent clauses."],["'Therefore' signals:",["result","example","time"],0,"Therefore marks a conclusion or consequence."],["Choose the subordinator.",["however","although","therefore"],1,"Although introduces a dependent clause."]],"related":["091","169","202","216"]},"130":{"intro":"Relative where, when and whose help attach descriptions of place, time and possession to the noun they modify.","sections":[{"title":"Where, when, whose","explain":"Relative words can replace a longer expression with a preposition, creating more natural sentences.","points":["the place where we met = the place in which we met.","the day when we moved = the day on which we moved.","the employee whose badge was missing = the employee's badge was missing.","Whose can sometimes refer to things in formal language: a company whose products are trusted.","That or zero relative may be possible after time nouns in some defining clauses: the day (that) we met."],"examples":[["This is the café where we first met.","Where = at which."],["I remember the day when the course began.","When = on which."],["She met a man whose brother is a pilot.","Possessive relative."]]},{"title":"Avoid redundant prepositions","explain":"Use one structure rather than duplicating the locative or temporal relation.","points":["the office where I work (not where I work in).","the office in which I work (formal alternative).","the day when I arrived (not when I arrived on).","The reason why is acceptable, though the reason that/zero is often shorter."],"examples":[["The office in which she works is nearby.","Formal equivalent."],["That is the reason I left.","Omitted relative marker."]]}],"mistakes":[["the place where I work in","the place where I work","Where already encodes place relation."],["the woman which car was stolen","the woman whose car was stolen","Whose expresses possession."],["the day when we arrived on","the day when we arrived","Remove redundant on."]],"quiz":[["For possession use:",["whose","where","when"],0,"Whose."],["For a location use:",["where","whose","if"],0,"Where."],["Choose a non-redundant clause.",["the office where she works","the office where she works in","the office which where she works"],0,"One location relation suffices."]],"related":["090","113","114"]},"132":{"intro":"An indirect question embeds a question inside a polite sentence. It uses subject–verb order, not direct question inversion.","sections":[{"title":"Polite question openings","explain":"Use Could you tell me...?, Do you know...?, I'd like to know... and I wonder... . The embedded part uses statement word order.","points":["Where is the bank? → Could you tell me where the bank is?","When does the train leave? → Do you know when the train leaves?","Is the office open? → Could you tell me if the office is open?","Don't use do/does/did twice; the embedded clause has its own normal structure.","If the whole outer sentence is a question, end it with a question mark."],"examples":[["Could you tell me where the nearest station is?","Embedded subject before is."],["Do you know whether the appointment is confirmed?","Whether introduces a yes/no clause."],["I wonder why the package was delayed.","Polite statement, no final question mark."]]},{"title":"Indirect questions versus reported questions","explain":"A reported question describes something someone asked in the past. A polite indirect question asks something now. Both use embedded statement order.","points":["Polite: Could you tell me where she works?","Reported: He asked where she worked.","Wh-word remains; yes/no questions use if/whether.","In formal writing, whether is often preferred before or not."],"examples":[["Could you tell me what time the clinic opens?","Indirect present question."],["She asked what time the clinic opened.","Reported past question."]]}],"mistakes":[["Could you tell me where is it?","Could you tell me where it is?","No embedded inversion."],["Do you know does he work?","Do you know if he works?","Use if and remove does."],["I wonder where does it go.","I wonder where it goes.","Statement word order after wonder."]],"quiz":[["Choose the polite question.",["Could you tell me where is the office?","Could you tell me where the office is?","Could you tell me where does office is?"],1,"Embedded subject–verb order."],["For yes/no indirect questions use:",["if/whether","because","which always"],0,"Use if/whether."],["'I wonder where she lives' ends with:",["usually a full stop","always an exclamation mark","a question mark is mandatory"],0,"It is a declarative statement."]],"related":["038","116","131"]},"137":{"intro":"Present perfect continuous highlights duration, recent activity and a present result of an ongoing or recently finished process.","sections":[{"title":"Form, timeframe and effect","explain":"Use have/has been + -ing. The action began before now and continues or has just stopped with a present connection.","points":["I have been waiting for twenty minutes.","She has been studying since six o'clock.","Why are you wet? I have been walking in the rain.","Have you been working all day?","Use the past perfect continuous to locate the duration before a past time, not now."],"examples":[["I've been learning English for a year.","Duration continuing to now."],["She's been repairing the laptop all afternoon.","Ongoing activity."],["Their eyes are tired because they've been reading.","Recent activity explains the result."]]},{"title":"Simple perfect versus continuous","explain":"The continuous is not always better for long duration. Counted finished outcomes, states and permanent truths often favour the simple.","points":["I've read six articles (total completed) versus I've been reading articles (activity).","I've known her since school (know is normally stative).","It's been raining all night (ongoing activity) versus It has rained twice this week (count).","Be careful with verbs like live/work: both are possible in several contexts."],"examples":[["He has completed four tasks.","Finished total."],["He has been working on one task all morning.","Emphasis on effort and duration."]]}],"mistakes":[["He has been finish the work.","He has been finishing the work.","Continuous form needs -ing, though completed outcome often uses has finished."],["We have been know each other.","We have known each other.","Know usually stative."],["I've been read three books.","I've read three books.","Completed counted total."]],"quiz":[["Form:",["have been + -ing","has being + -ing","have + base verb"],0,"Perfect continuous."],["Choose a total result.",["I've finished four reports.","I've been finishing four reports (completed total).","I have finishing."],0,"Simple perfect often totals."],["Choose continuing activity.",["She has been practising since lunch.","She had practised tomorrow.","She is practising yesterday."],0,"Since + current continuing activity."]],"related":["073","075","097","138"]},"148":{"intro":"The third conditional considers a different past: something did not happen, and we imagine a different result.","sections":[{"title":"If + past perfect, would have + participle","explain":"The structure describes an unreal past condition and its unreal past consequence. Could have and might have can convey alternative degrees of possibility.","points":["If I had left earlier, I would have caught the train.","If she had studied, she might have passed.","Past perfect in if-clause: had + past participle.","Result: would/could/might + have + past participle.","The form is about an imagined alternative, not evidence that the alternative really happened."],"examples":[["If we had booked earlier, we would have saved money.","We did not book earlier."],["If he had called, I could have helped.","He did not call, so help was unavailable."],["If they had checked the address, the parcel might have arrived.","Alternative possible outcome."]]},{"title":"Meaning, regret and inverted conditionals","explain":"Third conditionals often express regret, criticism or relief. Formal English may omit if through subject–auxiliary inversion.","points":["If I had known = Had I known (formal inversion).","She would have been upset if you had said that (negative potential outcome).","Do not use would have in the ordinary if-clause for a basic third conditional.","Mixed conditionals connect a past condition to a present result; see Lesson 149."],"examples":[["Had I known about the delay, I would have waited.","Formal inverted conditional."],["If it had not rained, we could have walked.","Imagined past change."]]}],"mistakes":[["If I would have known, I would help.","If I had known, I would have helped.","Past perfect + would have."],["If she had went","if she had gone","Past participle of go is gone."],["Had I knew","Had I known","Inversion requires had + participle."]],"quiz":[["Choose a third conditional.",["If she calls, I'll answer.","If she had called, I would have answered.","If she called, I'd answer."],1,"Unreal past."],["Choose the past perfect.",["had gone","had went","has go"],0,"Had + participle."],["Formal inverted 'If I had known' is:",["Had I known","Did I had known","Were I known"],0,"Had precedes subject."]],"related":["086","109","149","178"]},"153":{"intro":"Complex passive forms include continuous, perfect, infinitive and -ing passives. Good writing uses them only when the receiver or process deserves attention.","sections":[{"title":"Passive forms across tense and aspect","explain":"The main lexical verb remains a past participle. Auxiliary combinations show whether the process is ongoing, complete or in another time frame.","points":["Present continuous: is/are being + participle — The road is being repaired.","Past continuous: was/were being + participle — The road was being repaired.","Present perfect: has/have been + participle — The road has been repaired.","Past perfect: had been + participle — The road had been repaired.","Future perfect: will have been + participle — The road will have been repaired.","Some highly complex passive forms are technically possible but rare and awkward; rephrase when clarity suffers."],"examples":[["The bridge is being inspected.","Ongoing process."],["The bridge has been inspected.","Completed action relevant to now."],["The bridge had been inspected before the storm.","Completion before a past point."]]},{"title":"Agents, verbs with two objects and impersonal style","explain":"A by phrase names an agent; a with phrase often names an instrument. Some verbs with two objects permit different passive focuses.","points":["The door was opened by the caretaker (agent).","The door was opened with a key (instrument).","They gave Sara an award → Sara was given an award / An award was given to Sara.","Avoid passive when hiding responsibility would be misleading.","Be precise with be vs get passives: He got injured is a common informal event-focused passive."],"examples":[["The information was provided to the team.","Focus on what was provided."],["Sara was given an award.","Indirect object becomes subject."]]}],"mistakes":[["The report is being write.","The report is being written.","Continuous passive needs being + participle."],["The report has been wrote.","The report has been written.","Written is the participle."],["The door was opened by a key.","The door was opened with a key.","With identifies an instrument; by often identifies an agent."]],"quiz":[["Present continuous passive:",["is being repaired","is been repaired","is repairing by"],0,"Is being + participle."],["Present perfect passive:",["has being cleaned","has been cleaned","had clean"],1,"Has been + participle."],["An instrument is commonly introduced by:",["by","with","despite"],1,"With a key; by a person."]],"related":["094","118","119","154","200"]},"154":{"intro":"Reporting passives are common in news reports and formal writing when information is attributed to people or sources without naming every speaker.","sections":[{"title":"It is said that ...","explain":"An impersonal reporting passive uses it + passive reporting verb + that-clause. This reports a claim without claiming firsthand evidence.","points":["It is said that the building is historic.","It was reported that several roads had closed.","It is believed that the system is reliable.","Common verbs: say, believe, think, report, expect, know, consider.","'It is said that...' is not a guarantee that the statement is true; check sources in factual writing."],"examples":[["It is reported that the service will reopen.","Attribution without a named reporter."],["It was believed that the plan would work.","Past viewpoint."]]},{"title":"Personal reporting passives","explain":"The subject of the reported clause may become the main subject followed by a passive reporting verb and a to-infinitive. Perfect infinitives often refer to earlier events.","points":["People say the actor lives here → The actor is said to live here.","People believe he left yesterday → He is believed to have left yesterday.","People expect the train to arrive late → The train is expected to arrive late.","Use to be + -ing for a current process when appropriate: She is believed to be working abroad.","Prefer direct attribution to a named source if the claim matters or might be contested."],"examples":[["The company is thought to employ 200 people.","Current report: to employ."],["The minister is reported to have resigned.","Earlier event: to have resigned."],["The guests are expected to arrive soon.","Future expectation."]]}],"mistakes":[["He is said that he lives here.","He is said to live here.","Personal pattern uses to-infinitive."],["He is believed to left.","He is believed to have left.","Earlier event needs perfect infinitive."],["It is said he to be rich.","It is said that he is rich.","Impersonal pattern uses a that-clause."]],"quiz":[["Choose the personal reporting passive.",["She is believed to be abroad.","She believes to abroad.","She is believed that abroad."],0,"Passive reporting verb + to-infinitive."],["Earlier event uses:",["to have done","to did","to doing"],0,"Perfect infinitive."],["'It is reported that ...' primarily:",["attributes a claim","proves a claim","expresses a command"],0,"Reporting is not independent verification."]],"related":["115","153","156","200"]},"156":{"intro":"Advanced reported speech requires careful reference to who said what, when and where. Mechanical backshift alone is not enough.","sections":[{"title":"Tense backshift with context","explain":"Reported speech often uses past reporting verbs and backshifted tenses, but the original tense can survive when the statement is still true, the context is immediate or the reporting verb is present.","points":["'I work in Doha' → He said he worked in Doha (past viewpoint).","He says he works in Doha (present reporting verb, no automatic backshift).","She said she had already completed it (present perfect or past simple may backshift to past perfect).","Past perfect and modal would, could, should often stay unchanged.","Where the original words are quoted, do not change their tense inside the quotation."],"examples":[["He explained that he had left before the meeting began.","Earlier past requires past perfect."],["The lecturer said that light travels faster than sound.","An enduring fact may remain in present simple."],["She has just said that she needs help.","Current reporting context."]]},{"title":"Pronouns, time and location","explain":"Time expressions change only if the reporter's position makes the old reference inappropriate. Do not replace every now or here automatically.","points":["today → that day; tomorrow → the following/next day; yesterday → the previous day.","last week → the week before; next month → the following month.","here → there, this → that only when location/demonstrative viewpoint changes.","I, we, you, my and our must be recast to fit speaker and listener identities.","Be careful with conditionals: past forms in unreal conditions often remain past."],"examples":[["On Tuesday, he said he would return the following day.","Following day refers to Wednesday."],["Speaking from the same office: She said she was happy here.","Here may stay when the place is unchanged."]]}],"mistakes":[["She said she will call yesterday.","She said she would call the previous day.","Time and sequence must be coherent."],["He said he would be here (reporter is elsewhere).","He said he would be there.","Here/there depends on reporter's location."],["The teacher said the Earth was round (claimed no longer true).","The teacher said the Earth is round.","Backshift is allowed, but keeping present can clarify an enduring truth."]],"quiz":[["After 'He says ...' in the present:",["backshift is always required","backshift is not automatically required","all verbs become past perfect"],1,"Present reporting need not backshift."],["'Tomorrow' from a later reporting viewpoint may become:",["the following day","the day before","a year ago"],0,"It shifts forward relative to the original speech."],["The word 'here' changes to 'there':",["always","only when the viewpoint requires it","never"],1,"Reference changes depend on context."]],"related":["115","116","117","157"]},"157":{"intro":"Reporting verbs show attitude and intention: admit, deny, apologise, promise, suggest, recommend, insist, warn and encourage. Each takes a different grammatical pattern.","sections":[{"title":"Verb + grammatical pattern","explain":"Learn the reporting verb with its complement. A grammatically correct tense is not enough if the verb pattern is wrong.","points":["admit + -ing / admit that: She admitted making a mistake.","deny + -ing / deny that: He denied taking the file.","promise + to-infinitive / promise that: She promised to call.","agree + to-infinitive / agree that: He agreed to help.","suggest/recommend + -ing or that-clause: She suggested waiting.","warn/advise/encourage + object + (not) to-infinitive: She warned me not to enter."],"examples":[["He admitted making the error.","Admit + -ing."],["She denied that she had received the email.","Deny + that-clause."],["They promised to investigate.","Promise + to-infinitive."]]},{"title":"Meaning, responsibility and tone","explain":"Reporting verbs are not neutral substitutes for say. Claim indicates an assertion that may be disputed; admit suggests something unwelcome; insist means emphatic assertion or demand.","points":["He claimed that the figures were correct (speaker attribution, not verification).","She admitted that the figures were wrong (acknowledgement).","He insisted on checking the figures (insist on + -ing).","The officer reminded us to bring identification (reminder).","Distinguish 'suggest that we leave' from the incorrect 'suggested us to leave'."],"examples":[["She apologised for arriving late.","Apologise for + -ing."],["They urged us to act immediately.","Urge + object + to-infinitive."]]}],"mistakes":[["He denied to take it.","He denied taking it.","Deny normally takes -ing or a that-clause."],["She suggested us to wait.","She suggested that we wait.","Suggest does not normally take object + to-infinitive."],["They insisted to check.","They insisted on checking.","Insist on + -ing."]],"quiz":[["She admitted ____ the file.",["lose","losing","to lose"],1,"Admit + -ing."],["He promised ____ tomorrow.",["calling","call","to call"],2,"Promise + to-infinitive."],["Choose the standard construction.",["She warned me not to go.","She warned me don't go.","She warned me for not go."],0,"Warn + object + not to-infinitive."]],"related":["115","117","156","199"]},"161":{"intro":"Advanced article use distinguishes abstract qualities, institutional roles, generalisations and specific types of nouns in professional English.","sections":[{"title":"Abstract and generic reference","explain":"Mass abstract nouns often appear without articles for general concepts but take the when specified by context. A/an is sometimes possible for a type or instance.","points":["Freedom matters (general). The freedom we enjoy is valuable (specific).","Experience is important (knowledge); It was an experience (one event).","Life is unpredictable (general); the life of a traveller (specific life).","The rich, the elderly and the unemployed refer to groups of people; use respectful, precise language."],"examples":[["Trust is essential in healthcare.","General abstract concept."],["The trust between colleagues matters.","Specific relationship."],["She showed a kindness I will never forget.","One striking instance of a quality."]]},{"title":"Institutional and geographical patterns","explain":"Sometimes dropping the means participating in an institution's normal role rather than focusing on its building. Geography follows many conventions.","points":["in hospital (as patient, BrE); at the hospital (location).","go to prison (as prisoner) versus go to the prison (visit the building).","at sea; by air; on foot (fixed expressions).","the Alps, the Pacific, the Thames; but Mount Everest, Lake Geneva.","the UK, the UAE; but Qatar, France."],"examples":[["The visitor went to the hospital.","Location-specific visit."],["He is at sea for six months.","Fixed expression."]]}],"mistakes":[["The honesty is valuable (general).","Honesty is valuable.","Abstract general reference."],["the Mount Everest","Mount Everest","Mountain names usually have zero article with Mount."],["She went to prison to visit her brother (if visitor).","She went to the prison to visit her brother.","The article identifies a visit to the building."]],"quiz":[["Choose general abstract reference.",["Patience is important.","The patience is important (general).","A patience is important."],0,"Zero article for the general quality."],["Which takes 'the'?",["Pacific Ocean","Mount Everest","Qatar"],0,"The Pacific Ocean."],["'Go to prison' often means:",["be imprisoned","tour the building","visit an officer"],0,"Institutional role expression."]],"related":["027","067","123","219"]},"163":{"intro":"Adjectives ending in -ed and -ing often look similar but tell us different things: someone's feeling and the thing that causes that feeling.","sections":[{"title":"Feeling versus cause","explain":"The -ed adjective usually tells us how a person feels. The -ing adjective tells us what makes somebody feel that way. Both can describe a person, but with different meanings.","points":["I am interested = I feel interest. I am interesting = other people find me interesting.","She was confused by the question; the question was confusing.","The -ed form can describe an affected group or object, not only a person: damaged equipment."],"examples":[["I am excited about the new project.","The person experiences excitement."],["The new project is exciting.","The project causes excitement."],["He looked disappointed.","His expression showed a feeling."]]},{"title":"Frequently confused pairs","explain":"Practise in complete sentences. The same noun may be described from two viewpoints.","points":["bored / boring; tired / tiring; worried / worrying","frightened / frightening; annoyed / annoying; satisfied / satisfying","amazed / amazing; interested / interesting; relaxed / relaxing"],"examples":[["The safety briefing was reassuring.","It caused reassurance."],["The families felt reassured.","The people experienced reassurance."]]}],"mistakes":[["I am boring in class.","I am bored in class.","Bored is the feeling; boring means you cause boredom."],["The trip was exhausted.","The trip was exhausting.","An exhausting trip makes people tired."],["We were exciting about it.","We were excited about it.","People feeling excitement are excited."]],"quiz":[["The documentary was ____.",["fascinated","fascinating","fascinate"],1,"The film caused fascination."],["After twelve hours I felt ____.",["exhausted","exhausting","exhaust"],0,"The person feels exhausted."],["Which is correct?",["The news was worrying.","The news was worried.","The news were worry."],0,"Worrying describes the news as a cause of concern."]],"related":["030","164"]},"164":{"intro":"Some adjectives have degrees (cold, tired), while others express an absolute or extreme idea (freezing, impossible). Choose intensifiers to match the type.","sections":[{"title":"Gradable adjectives","explain":"Gradable adjectives can express different strengths. Combine them with slightly, fairly, quite, rather, very or extremely, depending on meaning and tone.","points":["slightly cold → fairly cold → very cold → extremely cold.","Quite can mean fairly in many contexts; with some absolute adjectives it means completely.","Too + adjective expresses excess or a problem: too expensive; very + adjective simply describes a high degree."],"examples":[["The soup is slightly warm.","Slightly marks a small degree."],["This answer is much clearer.","Comparative intensified by much."],["The room is too small for six people.","Too suggests a problem."]]},{"title":"Extreme and non-gradable adjectives","explain":"Extreme adjectives already contain a strong meaning. In standard teaching usage, absolutely or completely often works better than very. Real usage is flexible, so focus on natural combinations rather than rigid bans.","points":["freezing, boiling, exhausted, terrified, delighted often prefer absolutely.","impossible, perfect, unique, dead are often treated as non-gradable, especially in formal style.","Really works with many adjective types: really tired, really amazing."],"examples":[["The view was absolutely stunning.","Absolutely naturally strengthens an extreme adjective."],["We were completely exhausted.","Completely works with exhausted."]]}],"mistakes":[["very freezing","absolutely freezing","Absolutely is the usual intensifier with freezing."],["too perfect to work","too complicated to work","Perfect does not normally mean excessively difficult."],["very more useful","much more useful","Comparatives take much/far/a lot, not very."]],"quiz":[["Choose the natural combination.",["absolutely exhausted","very exhaustedest","much exhausted"],0,"Absolutely matches an extreme adjective."],["Too noisy means:",["pleasantly loud","excessively noisy","slightly quiet"],1,"Too signals excess."],["Choose the natural comparison.",["very more reliable","far more reliable","most more reliable"],1,"Far can intensify a comparative."]],"related":["031","061","163","165"]},"165":{"intro":"Where you place an adverb can change meaning. Advanced learners must distinguish time, manner, focus, attitude and the scope of only, even, almost and just.","sections":[{"title":"Adverb position and meaning","explain":"Many adverbs of manner follow the verb or its object. Frequency adverbs often sit mid-clause. Sentence adverbs may appear at the front and be separated by a comma.","points":["She completed the task carefully (manner at the end).","She has always completed the task carefully (frequency after auxiliary).","Unfortunately, the service was cancelled (speaker's attitude).","Avoid separating a verb from its direct object with a manner adverb: say 'read the report carefully', not 'read carefully the report'."],"examples":[["He carefully checked the equipment.","Carefully emphasises the careful approach."],["He checked the equipment carefully.","A neutral end-position manner adverb."],["Apparently, the order has arrived.","Apparently indicates reported or inferred information."]]},{"title":"Focus adverbs: only, even, almost, just","explain":"A focus adverb attaches to the part of the sentence it limits or highlights. Moving it can change what the statement claims.","points":["Only Sara checked the list: nobody else did.","Sara only checked the list: the action was limited to checking.","Sara checked only the list: she checked nothing else.","Almost usually means nearly, and must attach to the right phrase."],"examples":[["I almost sent the email.","I did not send it, but came close."],["I sent almost every email.","Most emails were sent; a few were not."]]}],"mistakes":[["I explained clearly the rule.","I explained the rule clearly.","This is the usual neutral object + manner order."],["Only I said the answer, so I said only the answer.","I alone gave the answer; I gave only the answer.","These focus meanings are different; separate them."],["He very speaks clearly.","He speaks very clearly.","Very modifies clearly, not the verb directly."]],"quiz":[["'Only Ben called' means:",["Ben was the sole caller.","Ben did nothing except call.","Ben called just one person."],0,"Only scopes over Ben."],["Choose neutral word order.",["She read carefully the note.","She read the note carefully.","She the note carefully read."],1,"Manner follows the object."],["'Apparently' often signals:",["reported or inferred information","certainty from direct proof","strong prohibition"],0,"It marks source or stance."]],"related":["031","039","064","172"]},"167":{"intro":"English prepositions form patterns with nouns, adjectives and verbs. Memorise the whole chunk instead of translating a single preposition from your first language.","sections":[{"title":"Noun, verb and adjective collocations","explain":"A collocation is a natural word combination. Some common prepositional chunks are predictable; others must be learned together.","points":["depend on; rely on; listen to; belong to; apologise for; suffer from","a reason for; an interest in; access to; a solution to; an increase in","proud of; keen on; aware of; interested in; responsible for","verb + object + preposition: remind someone of something; provide someone with something."],"examples":[["We depend on accurate information.","Depend takes on."],["She apologised for arriving late.","For + -ing."],["We need a solution to the problem.","Solution usually takes to."]]},{"title":"Prepositions at the end of questions and clauses","explain":"It is common and grammatical in modern English to leave a preposition at the end of a natural question or relative clause. Very formal writing may move it to the front.","points":["Who are you speaking to? (natural spoken English).","The colleague I spoke to was helpful (natural relative clause).","To whom did you speak? (formal).","Do not add an extra preposition where a transitive verb already takes a direct object: discuss the plan, not discuss about the plan."],"examples":[["What are you looking for?","The preposition is naturally stranded."],["This is the person I wrote to.","An omitted relative pronoun is possible."]]}],"mistakes":[["discuss about the plan","discuss the plan","Discuss takes a direct object."],["married with a teacher","married to a teacher","Married to is standard."],["listen music","listen to music","Listen is usually followed by to."]],"quiz":[["Choose the correct combination.",["depend of","depend on","depend for"],1,"Depend on."],["Which is a natural question?",["Who are you waiting for?","For who you wait?","For what are you waiting for?"],0,"A preposition may occur at the end."],["Choose the standard phrase.",["access to information","access for information","access of information"],0,"Access to."]],"related":["126","022","068","198"]},"168":{"intro":"Advanced phrasal-verb accuracy depends on particles, separability, stress, register and whether the expression is literal or idiomatic.","sections":[{"title":"Three common structures","explain":"Distinguish intransitive particles, separable particle verbs and inseparable prepositional verbs.","points":["Intransitive: The engine broke down.","Separable: hand in the form / hand the form in / hand it in.","Prepositional: look into the issue / look into it (not look it into).","Three-word: put up with noise / put up with it (tolerate).","In some expressions the particle changes the meaning completely: take in, take out, take over, take up."],"examples":[["They carried out an investigation.","Carry out = perform."],["Please hand it in by Friday.","Pronoun before in."],["We will look into the complaint.","Look into is inseparable."]]},{"title":"Register and precise meaning","explain":"Many phrasal verbs are neutral and acceptable in professional English; some are informal. Replace only when the context demands it.","points":["find out → discover (often more formal).","put off → postpone (a neutral/formal alternative).","carry out → conduct (both widely used professionally).","bring up = mention a topic; take up = occupy time or begin a hobby.","Never assume a one-word synonym has exactly the same meaning in every context."],"examples":[["Please follow up on the report.","Follow up on = pursue/check progress."],["The committee will draw up new guidelines.","Draw up = prepare formally."]]}],"mistakes":[["hand in it","hand it in","Pronoun between separable verb and particle."],["look the case into","look into the case","Prepositional verb remains together."],["put with up the noise","put up with the noise","Three-word verb keeps its order."]],"quiz":[["Choose the correct sentence.",["Hand it in.","Hand in it.","Hand it."],0,"Separable pronoun placement."],["'Look into' means:",["investigate","stare at only","stop"],0,"Look into an issue = investigate."],["'Draw up guidelines' means:",["prepare","tear","ignore"],0,"Draw up = formulate."]],"related":["127","209","222"]},"169":{"intro":"In longer writing, connect reasons, contrasts, unexpected results and consequences precisely. Learn both meaning and grammar pattern.","sections":[{"title":"Four essential logical relationships","explain":"Cause explains why; result explains what happened; contrast compares different ideas; concession acknowledges a fact despite which another statement remains true.","points":["Cause: because + clause; because of + noun phrase/-ing.","Result: so + clause; therefore + new sentence/clause with proper punctuation.","Contrast: whereas/while + clause; in contrast + separate statement.","Concession: although/even though + clause; despite/in spite of + noun/-ing."],"examples":[["The road was closed because of flooding.","Because of + noun."],["Although it was costly, the upgrade was essential.","Unexpected conclusion."],["The first option is cheaper, whereas the second is faster.","Direct contrast."]]},{"title":"Avoid mixed grammatical patterns","explain":"One connector may require a full subject–verb clause; another needs a noun phrase. Choosing the correct shape matters as much as choosing the correct meaning.","points":["because she was absent (clause) vs because of her absence (noun phrase).","despite the rain (noun) vs although it was raining (clause).","due to / owing to often introduce a noun phrase; usage varies across styles.","As a result is a discourse link; it usually starts a new sentence or follows a semicolon."],"examples":[["Despite being tired, she completed the work.","Despite + -ing."],["Because she was tired, she took a short break.","Because + full clause."]]}],"mistakes":[["despite she was tired","although she was tired","Despite is followed by a noun/-ing, not normally a full finite clause."],["because of it was late","because it was late","Because introduces a clause; because of takes a noun phrase."],["He was ill. For this reason, because he left.","He was ill. For this reason, he left.","Avoid redundant connectors."]],"quiz":[["Choose the correct phrase.",["despite the rain","despite it rained","despite of rain"],0,"Despite takes a noun phrase."],["Choose a result connector.",["whereas","consequently","even though"],1,"Consequently introduces a result."],["'Because of' is usually followed by:",["a noun phrase","a finite clause only","a modal verb"],0,"Because of the delay."]],"related":["091","129","202","216"]},"198":{"intro":"A preposition is usually followed by a noun phrase or an -ing clause, not a bare verb or an ordinary to-infinitive. This pattern explains many advanced verb combinations.","sections":[{"title":"Preposition + -ing","explain":"When a verb follows a genuine preposition, use the -ing form. This includes complex patterns where the word to is a preposition rather than an infinitive marker.","points":["interested in learning; worried about missing; good at solving","look forward to meeting you; be used to working nights","object to paying; apologise for being late","without checking; after finishing; before leaving","Exception: to + bare verb forms an infinitive when to is an infinitive marker: want to leave, need to study."],"examples":[["I look forward to hearing from you.","To is a preposition; hearing is -ing."],["She is used to working early shifts.","Used to + -ing means accustomed to."],["He left without saying goodbye.","Without + -ing."]]},{"title":"Meaning-sensitive verb patterns","explain":"Some verbs accept different complements with different meanings; others take a fixed pattern. Prepositions help clarify the relationship between ideas.","points":["stop doing = cease an activity; stop to do = pause another activity in order to do something.","remember doing = recall an earlier action; remember to do = not forget a future duty.","insist on doing; succeed in doing; prevent someone from doing.","There is no general rule that an -ing form always means past and an infinitive always means future; learn each verb pattern."],"examples":[["She stopped smoking.","She gave up the habit."],["She stopped to answer the phone.","She paused another activity in order to answer."]]}],"mistakes":[["look forward to meet you","look forward to meeting you","To is a preposition here."],["good at solve problems","good at solving problems","At + -ing."],["insist to pay","insist on paying","Insist on + -ing is a common pattern."]],"quiz":[["Complete: I look forward to ____ you.",["meet","meeting","met"],1,"Look forward to + -ing."],["Complete: She apologised for ____ late.",["be","being","to be"],1,"For + -ing."],["Stop to rest means:",["stop resting permanently","pause another action to rest","remember a past rest"],1,"The infinitive expresses purpose."]],"related":["089","124","126","167"]},"200":{"intro":"Master the passive system across tenses, aspects, modal meanings, reporting constructions and non-finite clauses—and choose it for a clear reason.","sections":[{"title":"Passive form map","explain":"Use be + past participle; change the auxiliaries to express tense or aspect. Identify the event time and focus before selecting a form.","points":["Present simple: is done; past simple: was done; future: will be done.","Present continuous: is being done; past continuous: was being done.","Present perfect: has been done; past perfect: had been done.","Future perfect: will have been done.","Modal: must be done; modal perfect: should have been done.","Non-finite: to be done; being done; having been done (formal/advanced)."],"examples":[["The checks are completed each morning.","Habitual present passive."],["The checks are being completed now.","Ongoing passive."],["The checks will have been completed by noon.","Completed before a future deadline."]]},{"title":"Choice, meaning and revision","explain":"The passive is useful for process descriptions, unknown agents and receiver-focused information. It can also make writing vague when responsibility should be clear.","points":["Use active when the actor matters: The committee approved the proposal.","Use passive when the outcome matters: The proposal was approved.","Keep the agent when essential: The proposal was approved by the safety committee.","Do not confuse stative adjective with event passive: The door is closed can describe a state; The door is being closed describes an ongoing action.","Avoid needless by-phrases when the agent is obvious.","Some verbs are intransitive and resist passivisation: arrive, happen, occur, sleep (without a suitable object)."],"examples":[["The policy was reviewed by an independent panel.","Agent matters for credibility."],["The results have been published.","Result and relevance matter more than who did it."]]}],"mistakes":[["The event was happened yesterday.","The event happened yesterday.","Happen is intransitive."],["The forms have being sent.","The forms have been sent.","Perfect passive uses been."],["The plan will have being finished.","The plan will have been finished.","Future perfect passive uses have been."]],"quiz":[["Future perfect passive:",["will have been completed","will be have completed","will have being complete"],0,"Will have been + participle."],["Choose the clearest active form when responsibility matters:",["The records were lost.","The office lost the records.","Records are losing."],1,"Identify the responsible actor if known and relevant."],["Which verb is generally not passivised?",["build","repair","happen"],2,"Happen has no direct object."]],"related":["094","118","119","153","154"]},"202":{"intro":"Concession means accepting a potentially opposing fact while keeping your main claim. This is essential for balanced professional and academic English.","sections":[{"title":"Although, even though, despite and in spite of","explain":"Although and even though are followed by finite clauses; despite and in spite of normally take a noun phrase, pronoun or -ing form.","points":["Although it was raining, we continued.","Even though he knew the risk, he agreed (often stronger surprise).","Despite the rain, we continued.","In spite of feeling tired, she worked.","Despite the fact that + clause is possible but wordier than although."],"examples":[["Even though the data were limited, the trend was clear.","Unexpected contrast."],["Despite the delay, the service improved.","Despite + noun phrase."],["In spite of working late, they met the deadline.","In spite of + -ing."]]},{"title":"Much as, while and nevertheless","explain":"Much as can introduce a formal concessive clause meaning although, particularly with liking or respecting. While may express contrast; nevertheless links independent statements.","points":["Much as I respect your view, I disagree.","While I accept the principle, I question the method.","The proposal is expensive. Nevertheless, it may save money later.","Don't use much as as a simple replacement for because."],"examples":[["Much as I admire the team, we need better evidence.","Respectful concession."],["The evidence was persuasive; nevertheless, concerns remain.","Linked independent clauses."]]}],"mistakes":[["despite of the delay","despite the delay","Despite does not take of; in spite of does."],["even though of the cost","even though it was costly","Even though needs a clause."],["Much as because I agree...","Although I agree...","Don't combine concession and reason connectors without logic."]],"quiz":[["Choose the correct construction.",["despite she was ill","despite her illness","despite of her illness"],1,"Despite + noun phrase."],["Which shows respectful concession?",["Much as I value your advice, I disagree.","Much as because I value...","Much for value..."],0,"Much as + clause is a formal concessive pattern."],["Concession means:",["accepting a point while contrasting it","listing only identical ideas","expressing a deadline"],0,"Concession acknowledges a counterpoint."]],"related":["091","129","169","216"]},"216":{"intro":"Complex writing uses coordination to join equal units and subordination to build a hierarchy of ideas. Understand the structure before adding punctuation.","sections":[{"title":"Coordination versus subordination","explain":"Coordinating conjunctions link elements with similar grammatical status. Subordinating conjunctions introduce an embedded clause that depends on another clause.","points":["Coordination: The report was clear, and the examples were useful.","Subordination: Because the report was clear, it was useful.","Correlative pairs: either...or; neither...nor; both...and; not only...but also.","Keep parallel forms: She likes reading, writing and speaking—not reading, to write and speaking."],"examples":[["Either you send an email or you call reception.","Parallel options."],["I stayed because I needed more information.","Dependent reason clause."],["The plan is both practical and affordable.","Balanced coordinated adjectives."]]},{"title":"Punctuation, ambiguity and clause boundaries","explain":"Use commas to make relations clear, especially around long or introductory dependent clauses. Avoid comma splices and fragments; long sentences are not automatically sophisticated.","points":["Comma splice: 'It was late, we left.' Better: 'It was late, so we left' or 'It was late; we left.'","A dependent clause alone is a fragment in standard writing: 'Although I agreed.' needs a main clause.","A restrictive clause normally has no comma: People who completed the course received a certificate.","A non-restrictive clause takes commas: My supervisor, who was helpful, explained the task."],"examples":[["Although the deadline was tight, the team delivered the project.","Subordinate clause first."],["We finished the analysis; however, further checks are needed.","Properly linked complete clauses."]]}],"mistakes":[["Although the meeting ended.","Although the meeting ended, we stayed.","A subordinated clause needs a main clause."],["We studied, we practised.","We studied, and we practised.","Fix a comma splice by adding a coordinator."],["Neither the director or the team agreed.","Neither the director nor the team agreed.","Use neither...nor."]],"quiz":[["A subordinate clause usually:",["can stand alone as a full sentence","depends on a main clause","never has a verb"],1,"Subordinate clauses depend on main clauses."],["Choose the correct pair.",["either...nor","neither...nor","both...or"],1,"Neither pairs with nor."],["Which avoids a comma splice?",["It rained, we waited.","It rained; we waited.","It rained we waited."],1,"A semicolon can join related independent clauses."]],"related":["091","113","129","169","202"]},"218":{"intro":"Subject–verb agreement depends on the grammatical head of the subject and the intended meaning, not just the nearest noun or surface plural ending.","sections":[{"title":"Core agreement and distractors","explain":"Find the head noun of the subject before choosing is/are, has/have, or a third-person singular verb. Interrupting phrases do not usually change the head.","points":["The manager of the teams is here (manager is head).","The teams in the building are here (teams is head).","Each/every/either/neither + singular noun often takes singular agreement: Each student has a ticket.","There is a problem; there are several problems.","The number of applicants is rising; a number of applicants are waiting."],"examples":[["The list of names is on the desk.","List controls is."],["Each of the students has an account.","Each is singular."],["A number of people are waiting.","A number of means several."]]},{"title":"Collective nouns and exceptional cases","explain":"British English often permits singular or plural agreement with collective nouns, depending on whether the group is seen as one unit or as its members. This is meaning-sensitive.","points":["The team is winning (one unit); the team are arguing among themselves (members, common BrE).","Statistics is a useful subject (field of study); the statistics are surprising (data).","The news is reassuring (news is singular).","Ten pounds is enough (one amount).","Neither of the answers is correct is standard formal usage; plural agreement is also found in everyday speech."],"examples":[["The committee are divided on the proposal.","British English: members seen individually."],["Mathematics is challenging for some learners.","Name of a subject is singular."]]}],"mistakes":[["The list of items are ready.","The list of items is ready.","Head noun list is singular."],["Every students have a book.","Every student has a book.","Every takes a singular count noun in this use."],["The news are bad.","The news is bad.","News normally takes singular agreement."]],"quiz":[["Complete: The number of visitors ____ increasing.",["is","are","have"],0,"The number is singular."],["Complete: A number of visitors ____ waiting.",["is","are","was"],1,"A number of means several."],["In British English, 'The team are ...' may be correct when:",["members are considered individually","team is an adjective","the speaker discusses one table"],0,"Notional plural agreement occurs with collectives."]],"related":["009","025","035","122"]},"219":{"intro":"At advanced level, articles and determiners often change focus, assumptions and shared knowledge rather than core grammaticality.","sections":[{"title":"A, the, zero and this/that","explain":"Article choice directs a reader's attention. An indefiniteness choice can introduce a new participant; definiteness assumes identifiable reference.","points":["I spoke to a colleague (new/unspecified). I spoke to the colleague you recommended (identified).","A certain colleague called (speaker may know which one but does not identify them).","This problem needs attention (proximal, currently salient); that problem (distal or contrastive).","The + singular generic noun can describe a species/class in formal prose: The tiger is endangered.","Plural zero generic often sounds more conversational: Tigers are endangered."],"examples":[["A solution was proposed, but the solution was rejected.","New then identifiable."],["This particular approach may be suitable.","Determiner and adjective narrow reference."],["Such a claim needs evidence.","Such a introduces a type of claim."]]},{"title":"Every, each, all, both and no","explain":"Determiners also determine how many members of a set a sentence talks about.","points":["Every employee has a badge (distributive, singular).","Each of the employees has a badge (focus on individuals).","All employees have badges (entire group).","Both proposals are practical (two). Neither proposal is practical (not either of two).","No employees were present (none).","Most information is useful (most + uncountable)."],"examples":[["Each answer reveals a different problem.","Individual members."],["All the information has been checked.","Information is singular/uncountable."]]}],"mistakes":[["Each employees have","Each employee has","Each + singular noun/verb here."],["an information","some information","Information is uncountable."],["The tigers are endangered (intended all tigers generically).","Tigers are endangered.","Zero plural is more neutral for generic statements."]],"quiz":[["For two options choose:",["both proposals","every proposals","much proposals"],0,"Both refers to two."],["Choose a general mass noun.",["Information helps.","An information helps.","Informations help."],0,"Information is mass."],["'The colleague you recommended' is:",["identified by the relative clause","necessarily unknown","automatically plural"],0,"A postmodifier identifies the referent."]],"related":["027","067","123","161","218"]},"224":{"intro":"British and American English share core grammar, but differ in spelling, pronunciation, vocabulary and some everyday patterns. Choose one consistent variety for formal work while understanding both.","sections":[{"title":"Spelling and vocabulary","explain":"Engli Vance Academy teaches standard British usage, while recognising that American alternatives are usually valid in American contexts.","points":["BrE colour, favourite, organise, centre, travelling; AmE color, favorite, organize, center, traveling.","BrE lift, flat, holiday, queue, petrol, trousers; AmE elevator, apartment, vacation, line, gasoline, pants.","BrE autumn (AmE fall), rubbish (AmE trash), biscuit (AmE cookie, meanings overlap imperfectly).","Not all -ise spellings are mandatory in British English: Oxford-style -ize is also acceptable for many verbs."],"examples":[["Please join the queue for the lift.","British vocabulary."],["Our favourite colour is blue.","British spelling."],["The centre is organising an event.","British-style combination."]]},{"title":"Grammar and pronunciation","explain":"Varieties differ in details rather than being separate languages. Listen for meaning, not only for an unfamiliar accent.","points":["BrE at the weekend; AmE on the weekend (both widely understood).","BrE in hospital (patient); AmE in the hospital.","BrE present perfect is often preferred with just/already/yet: I've just eaten; AmE also commonly uses I just ate.","Collective nouns may take plural verbs more often in BrE: The team are... (context dependent).","British and American pronunciation differ in rhoticity, vowel qualities and individual words; no single accent represents every speaker."],"examples":[["I've just finished the report.","A common British present-perfect preference."],["The committee are considering the proposal.","Possible British notional agreement."]]}],"mistakes":[["American English is incorrect.","American English is another standard variety.","Different standard varieties can both be correct."],["Organize is impossible in the UK.","Organize is accepted in some British styles.","Oxford spelling allows many -ize forms."],["Every British speaker sounds the same.","British accents vary by region and speaker.","Accent variation is normal."]],"quiz":[["British spelling of color:",["colour","colore","collour"],0,"Colour."],["A common British transport word:",["elevator","lift","gasoline"],1,"Lift."],["BrE 'at the weekend' corresponds commonly to AmE:",["on the weekend","with weekend","from the weekend always"],0,"Both express weekend time."]],"related":["041","218"]},"016":{"intro":"An adjective gives information about a noun or pronoun. Start with what a person or thing is like, then learn where descriptive words belong in a sentence.","sections":[{"title":"Two basic positions","explain":"An adjective can come before a noun (attributive position) or after a linking verb such as be, seem, look or feel (predicative position). Most everyday descriptive adjectives work in both places.","points":["Before a noun: adjective + noun. Do not add an article between the adjective and the noun.","After be: subject + be + adjective. You do not normally put a before the adjective when there is no noun.","Adjectives do not change form for singular, plural, masculine or feminine nouns."],"examples":[["It is a quiet office.","Quiet comes before office."],["The office is quiet.","Quiet follows is."],["They are helpful colleagues.","Helpful stays unchanged with a plural noun."]]},{"title":"Useful adjective order","explain":"When you need several adjectives before one noun, a natural order often moves from opinion to size, age, shape, colour, origin and material. This is a tendency, not a reason to use long strings of adjectives.","points":["Opinion normally comes before factual description: a lovely small café.","Use commas between similar adjectives; avoid unnecessary commas between different types.","Use an adjective, not an adverb, after linking verbs expressing a state."],"examples":[["She bought a beautiful blue dress.","Opinion comes before colour."],["The soup tastes delicious.","Delicious describes the soup, not the action of tasting."]]}],"mistakes":[["She is a friendly.","She is friendly.","A singular count noun is needed after a: She is a friendly person."],["They are importants.","They are important.","English adjectives have no plural -s."],["The room looks beautifully.","The room looks beautiful.","Look is a linking verb here."]],"quiz":[["Choose the correct sentence.",["My nurses are kind.","My nurses are kinds.","My nurses is kind."],0,"Adjectives do not have a plural ending."],["Which adjective order sounds natural?",["a red lovely bag","a lovely red bag","a bag lovely red"],1,"Opinion usually comes before colour."],["After a linking verb, choose:",["The patient seems calmly.","The patient seems calm.","The patient is a calm."],1,"Calm describes the patient's state."]],"related":["030","061","163","164"]},"030":{"intro":"Adjectives describe appearance, personality, age, quality, condition, size and feelings. Choose a precise adjective and place it where the reader expects it.","sections":[{"title":"Describe people respectfully","explain":"Separate observable facts from opinions. Tall, short-haired and grey-haired describe appearance; reliable, patient and considerate describe qualities. Avoid labels that are too personal or judgemental.","points":["For appearance: be + adjective or have + noun phrase: She is tall; she has curly hair.","For personality: be + adjective: He is considerate.","Use quite, rather, fairly and very to adjust strength; not all adjectives accept very."],"examples":[["Our new colleague is reliable.","The adjective describes a quality."],["She has short, curly hair.","Hair is a noun; short and curly describe it."],["The room is fairly spacious.","Fairly reduces the strength of spacious."]]},{"title":"Adjectives that end in -ed and -ing","explain":"Many pairs have different meanings. -ed commonly describes how a person feels; -ing describes what produces the feeling. These pairs are examined in greater depth in Lesson 163.","points":["I am interested in the course: my feeling.","The course is interesting: its effect on learners.","Some adjectives only occur before a noun or after a linking verb: use asleep after be, not usually before a noun."],"examples":[["I was bored during the meeting.","Bored is the speaker's feeling."],["It was a boring meeting.","Boring describes the meeting's effect."]]}],"mistakes":[["I have 40 years.","I am 40 years old.","English normally uses be to state age."],["The lesson is interested.","The lesson is interesting.","The lesson creates interest."],["an asleep baby","a sleeping baby","Asleep normally comes after a linking verb: The baby is asleep."]],"quiz":[["Which is natural?",["She has blue eyes.","She is blue eyes.","She is having blue eyes."],0,"Have describes physical features such as eye colour."],["Complete: I felt ____ after the exam.",["exhausting","exhausted","exhaust"],1,"Exhausted describes the feeling."],["Choose a respectful description.",["He is a lazy worker.","He arrived late twice this week.","He is always useless."],1,"A specific observation is more objective than a label."]],"related":["016","163","164"]},"061":{"intro":"A comparative adjective compares two people, things, places or situations. The spelling of the comparative depends on the adjective.","sections":[{"title":"Form the comparative","explain":"Many short adjectives add -er; most longer adjectives use more. Compare two things with than when the second item is stated.","points":["small → smaller; nice → nicer (add -r to words ending in -e).","big → bigger (double the final consonant after a short stressed vowel); happy → happier (y → i).","useful → more useful; interesting → more interesting.","Irregular: good → better; bad → worse; far → farther/further depending on meaning."],"examples":[["This chair is lighter than that one.","Light adds -er."],["The instructions are clearer now.","Clear forms clearer."],["This explanation is more useful than the previous one.","Use more with useful."]]},{"title":"Patterns and common meaning changes","explain":"A comparison can appear without than when the other item is understood. Much, far and a little can modify comparatives. Use less + adjective to compare a smaller amount of a quality.","points":["Do not use more + -er together: more easier is incorrect.","Use than, not then, for a comparison.","More and more / -er and -er describes progressive change."],"examples":[["The second task is much easier.","Much strengthens the comparative."],["The days are getting shorter and shorter.","Repeated comparative shows gradual change."]]}],"mistakes":[["more happier","happier","Do not combine more with an -er comparative."],["This one is better then that.","This one is better than that.","Than introduces the second item."],["more good","better","Good has an irregular comparative."]],"quiz":[["Choose the comparative of busy.",["busier","more busier","busyier"],0,"Change consonant + y to -ier."],["Complete: This route is ____ than the other.",["more short","shorter","shortest"],1,"Short → shorter."],["Choose the stronger comparison.",["far more effective","very more effective","much effective"],0,"Far can intensify more effective."]],"related":["062","063","064"]},"062":{"intro":"A superlative shows the highest or lowest degree within a group of three or more, or within a clearly understood set.","sections":[{"title":"Build superlative forms","explain":"Short adjectives usually take -est; longer ones usually use most. Superlatives generally use the, although the article is sometimes omitted in fixed or possessive patterns.","points":["small → smallest; large → largest; big → biggest; happy → happiest.","beautiful → most beautiful; practical → most practical.","Irregular: good → best; bad → worst; far → farthest/furthest.","Use in for places/groups and of for a set of items: the tallest in the team; the best of the three."],"examples":[["This is the safest entrance.","Safe adds -st after e."],["She is the most experienced nurse in the unit.","Most with a longer adjective."],["It was the worst delay this month.","Worst is irregular."]]},{"title":"Avoid false superlatives","explain":"A superlative is about a set; compare meaningfully. If comparing exactly two options, the comparative is usually more natural, although everyday speech sometimes uses a superlative for two.","points":["The best of all is a common pattern, but do not use most + -est together.","One of the + superlative + plural noun: one of the busiest wards.","By far can strengthen a superlative."],"examples":[["This is one of the busiest stations in London.","Plural stations follows one of the."],["It is by far the simplest method.","By far intensifies the superlative."]]}],"mistakes":[["the most easiest","the easiest","Never combine most and -est."],["one of the best student","one of the best students","One of the requires a plural noun."],["The fastest of the hospital","the fastest in the hospital","Use in for membership of a place or group."]],"quiz":[["Choose the correct form.",["most big","biggest","biggerest"],1,"Big doubles g: biggest."],["Which phrase is correct?",["one of the nicest people","one of the nicest person","one of nicest people"],0,"Use one of the + superlative + plural."],["Complete: the ____ interesting chapter.",["most","-est","more"],0,"Interesting takes most."]],"related":["061","063"]},"031":{"intro":"An adverb adds information about a verb, an adjective, another adverb, or a whole sentence. Not every adverb ends in -ly.","sections":[{"title":"What adverbs do","explain":"Think of four everyday jobs: manner (how), time (when), place (where) and degree (how much). Some adverbs comment on the whole message.","points":["Manner: speak clearly, work carefully, drive slowly.","Time/place: arrive yesterday, come here, leave soon.","Degree: very tired, really helpful, quite slowly.","Sentence comment: Fortunately, nobody was hurt."],"examples":[["Please speak slowly.","Slowly describes how the speaker should speak."],["She is extremely careful.","Extremely modifies the adjective careful."],["Fortunately, we found the keys.","Fortunately comments on the whole statement."]]},{"title":"Adjective or adverb?","explain":"Use an adjective to describe a noun; use a manner adverb to describe an action. Learn irregular forms too.","points":["quick → quickly; careful → carefully; happy → happily.","good (adjective) → well (usual adverb): She sings well.","fast, hard, late and early can be adverbs without -ly.","Hardly means almost not; it does not mean in a hard-working manner."],"examples":[["He is a good teacher, and he teaches well.","Good describes a noun; well describes the verb."],["She works hard but hardly ever complains.","Hard and hardly mean different things."]]}],"mistakes":[["He speaks fluent.","He speaks fluently.","Use an adverb for the way he speaks."],["She plays good.","She plays well.","Well is the usual adverb of good."],["I hardly worked yesterday.","I worked hard yesterday.","Hardly changes the meaning to almost not."]],"quiz":[["Which word completes 'She drives ____'?",["careful","carefully","care"],1,"A manner adverb modifies drives."],["Which word is an adverb without -ly?",["fast","friendly","lovely"],0,"Fast works as an adjective and an adverb."],["'Hardly ever' means:",["almost always","very vigorously","almost never"],2,"Hardly ever refers to very low frequency."]],"related":["039","064","165","164"]},"039":{"intro":"Frequency adverbs help you describe routines accurately: always, usually, often, sometimes, rarely and never.","sections":[{"title":"Normal word order","explain":"Frequency adverbs usually go before the main verb but after be. With an auxiliary, place them after the first auxiliary. These are useful defaults, not absolute rules for emphasis.","points":["Subject + adverb + main verb: I usually start at eight.","Subject + be + adverb: She is often early.","Auxiliary + adverb + main verb: We have never visited.","Never already makes the clause negative in meaning, so do not add not."],"examples":[["They often have lunch together.","Often precedes the main verb have."],["He is rarely absent.","Rarely follows is."],["I have always enjoyed languages.","Always follows the first auxiliary have."]]},{"title":"Other ways to express frequency","explain":"Use every day, once a week, twice a month and from time to time as useful time expressions. These usually go at the beginning or end of a clause.","points":["Every day is two words when it means each day; everyday is an adjective: everyday English.","Sometimes is one word, meaning on some occasions; some times has a different meaning.","Hardly ever means almost never."],"examples":[["We review the notes twice a week.","Frequency phrase at the end."],["Every day, I practise for ten minutes.","Time expression at the beginning."]]}],"mistakes":[["I go always to work.","I always go to work.","Always normally precedes the main verb."],["She always is ready.","She is always ready.","With be, put the adverb after be."],["I don't never practise.","I never practise.","Avoid double negatives in standard English."]],"quiz":[["Choose the usual order.",["She often is tired.","She is often tired.","She tired often is."],1,"Frequency adverb follows be."],["'Hardly ever' means:",["nearly never","every day","very quickly"],0,"It expresses low frequency."],["Choose the adjective form.",["every day activity","everyday activity","everydayly activity"],1,"Everyday is an adjective."]],"related":["031","064","165"]},"064":{"intro":"Adverbs of manner describe how actions happen; comparisons of adverbs tell us how two actions differ.","sections":[{"title":"Make adverbs of manner","explain":"Many adjectives form an adverb with -ly, but spelling changes and irregular forms matter. Avoid assuming every -ly word is an adverb.","points":["safe → safely; gentle → gently; easy → easily.","good → well; fast → fast; hard → hard.","Friendly and lovely are adjectives, not usual manner adverbs.","Use an adverb with an action verb: speak quietly; use an adjective with a linking verb: sound quiet."],"examples":[["She answered politely.","Politely describes answering."],["The system works well.","Well is an irregular adverb."],["The driver stopped suddenly.","Suddenly describes how the action happened."]]},{"title":"Compare actions","explain":"Some adverbs take -er/-est, but many -ly adverbs use more/most. The comparative may be followed by than.","points":["fast → faster → fastest; hard → harder → hardest.","carefully → more carefully → most carefully.","well → better → best; badly → worse → worst.","Avoid more quickly-er and other double forms."],"examples":[["She explains the process more clearly than I do.","More modifies an -ly adverb."],["This bus travels faster than the other one.","Fast takes -er."]]}],"mistakes":[["He drives careful.","He drives carefully.","The action needs a manner adverb."],["more faster","faster","Do not use both more and -er."],["She speaks more good.","She speaks better.","Well has irregular comparative better."]],"quiz":[["Choose the correct adverb.",["happyly","happily","happy"],1,"Change y to i before -ly."],["Comparative of carefully:",["carefullier","more carefully","most careful"],1,"Use more with an -ly adverb."],["After sound as a linking verb:",["The music sounds beautiful.","The music sounds beautifully.","The music sounds beauty."],0,"Beautiful describes the music."]],"related":["031","039","061","165"]},"022":{"intro":"Prepositions of place show position. Learn the physical picture first, then common extended uses.","sections":[{"title":"In, on and at for position","explain":"In usually means inside an area or enclosure; on means contact with a surface; at identifies a point or location. Real usage depends on how a place is conceptualised.","points":["in the room, in a box, in London, in a photograph","on the table, on the wall, on the floor, on a bus","at the door, at reception, at the station, at work","British English: in hospital can mean receiving treatment; at the hospital often means at that location."],"examples":[["The files are in the drawer.","The drawer is an enclosed space."],["The notice is on the door.","The notice is attached to a surface."],["Please meet me at the entrance.","Entrance is treated as a point."]]},{"title":"Under, below, above, over, next to, between and among","explain":"These words give more precise location. Under and over can imply vertical relation and sometimes coverage; below and above are often used for relative levels.","points":["The bag is under the desk: lower and directly related to the desk.","The light is above the desk: higher, not necessarily touching.","Between commonly identifies separate distinct people/things, including more than two; among suggests being within a group.","Opposite means facing on the other side; beside/next to means immediately near."],"examples":[["The pharmacy is opposite the bank.","They face each other across a space."],["The chair is between the desk and the window.","Two reference points."]]}],"mistakes":[["in the table","on the table","Use on for objects resting on its surface."],["between the people around me","among the people around me","Among is natural for an undifferentiated group."],["at the London","in London","A city is an area: in."]],"quiz":[["The keys are ____ the drawer.",["in","at","over"],0,"Inside an enclosed space."],["We waited ____ reception.",["on","at","into"],1,"At marks a place/point."],["The shop is ____ the bank (facing it).",["opposite","inside","through"],0,"Opposite means facing across."]],"related":["041","068","167"]},"041":{"intro":"Time prepositions are small but important. Use at for clock times and certain fixed phrases, on for days and dates, and in for longer periods.","sections":[{"title":"At, on and in for time","explain":"A reliable starting pattern is: at a specific time, on a day or date, in a month, year, season or longer period. There are important fixed expressions and British usage details.","points":["at 8 a.m., at noon, at midnight, at night, at the weekend (common British English).","on Monday, on 3 November, on my birthday, on Monday morning.","in July, in 2026, in winter, in the morning, in the evening.","No preposition with yesterday, tomorrow, next week, last month or every Friday."],"examples":[["The lesson starts at half past nine.","Clock time takes at."],["We meet on Thursday afternoon.","Specific day + part of day takes on."],["She moved to London in 2022.","Year takes in."]]},{"title":"By, until, since, for and during","explain":"These are not interchangeable. By indicates a deadline; until gives the endpoint of a continuing situation; since gives a starting point; for shows duration.","points":["Finish by Friday = no later than Friday.","Stay until Friday = continue staying up to Friday.","I have worked here since April = starting point with a time period extending to now.","I have studied for three months = duration.","During the meeting = inside that event; for two hours = duration length."],"examples":[["Please send it by 5 p.m.","The latest acceptable time."],["We stayed until six.","The action continued to six."]]}],"mistakes":[["in Monday","on Monday","A named day takes on."],["since three hours","for three hours","For expresses duration; since marks the start."],["during two hours","for two hours","For + duration."]],"quiz":[["The train leaves ____ 07:30.",["at","in","on"],0,"At + time."],["Submit the report ____ Friday (deadline).",["until","by","since"],1,"By means no later than."],["I've lived here ____ 2019.",["since","for","during"],0,"Since + starting point."]],"related":["022","068","074","098"]},"068":{"intro":"Prepositions of movement describe the path from one place to another. They differ from prepositions of static position.","sections":[{"title":"Direction and path","explain":"Use to for a destination, into for movement from outside to inside, out of for movement to the outside, and through for movement within and out of an enclosed space.","points":["go to the clinic; walk towards the entrance (direction without necessarily reaching it).","walk into the room; step out of the lift.","walk through the tunnel; walk across the road.","walk along the river; walk past the shop; walk around the park."],"examples":[["She walked into the office.","Movement crosses the boundary to inside."],["We drove through the tunnel.","The route passes through an enclosed space."],["Please walk across the road carefully.","Movement from one side to the other."]]},{"title":"Into or in? Across or through?","explain":"The difference is often movement versus position. In describes a location; into highlights movement to the interior. Across commonly crosses a surface or open area; through traverses an enclosed or bounded space.","points":["The children are in the garden (location). They ran into the garden (movement).","The train went through a tunnel, but the pedestrians walked across the square.","Arrive at a building/station or arrive in a city/country; normally do not say arrive to."],"examples":[["They ran up the stairs and into the hall.","Two consecutive movement paths."],["We arrived at the airport at noon.","Arrive at for a specific place."]]}],"mistakes":[["arrived to London","arrived in London","Use arrive in for cities/countries."],["entered into the room","entered the room","Enter is usually transitive without into."],["crossed through the street","crossed the street","Cross is a verb; across is a preposition/adverb."]],"quiz":[["They walked ____ the bridge to the other side.",["across","into","inside"],0,"Across signals from one side to the other."],["She entered ____ room.",["to the","the","into the"],1,"Enter is usually directly followed by its object."],["We arrived ____ Glasgow.",["to","at","in"],2,"Arrive in + a city."]],"related":["022","041","167"]},"091":{"intro":"Conjunctions connect words, phrases or clauses. The most important beginner distinction is joining equal ideas (and, but, or, so) and explaining dependent ideas (because, although, if, when).","sections":[{"title":"And, but, or and so","explain":"Coordinating conjunctions join grammatically similar elements. And adds; but contrasts; or offers a choice; so gives a consequence. Use sensible punctuation in longer sentences.","points":["and = addition: I read the instructions and checked the form.","but = contrast: The task was difficult, but we finished it.","or = choice: Call me or send an email.","so = result: It was late, so we left.","Do not confuse because (reason) with so (result)."],"examples":[["The bus was delayed, so I took a taxi.","The delay caused the decision."],["I wanted to attend, but I was ill.","But expresses contrast."],["Would you like tea or coffee?","Or gives alternatives."]]},{"title":"Because, although, when and if","explain":"Subordinating conjunctions introduce clauses that depend on a main clause. A dependent clause may appear first; a comma is then common, especially after a long opening clause.","points":["because = reason: We stayed inside because it was raining.","although = unexpected contrast: Although it was raining, we went out.","when = time: Call me when you arrive.","if = condition: If the office is open, we can go in.","Standard English normally does not double although with but in the same clause pairing."],"examples":[["Although she was tired, she finished the report.","The result contrasts with expectation."],["Call me if you need help.","If introduces a condition."]]}],"mistakes":[["Although it was late, but we continued.","Although it was late, we continued.","Do not combine although with but in this pattern."],["Because it rained, so we stayed inside.","Because it rained, we stayed inside.","Use one main connector, not both."],["I was hungry so because I ate.","I ate because I was hungry.","Because introduces the reason."]],"quiz":[["Choose the reason connector.",["because","so","but"],0,"Because introduces a reason."],["Which sentence is standard?",["Although the test was hard, but she passed.","Although the test was hard, she passed.","Although the test was hard, so she passed."],1,"No but or so is needed."],["'Or' most often expresses:",["contrast","choice","reason"],1,"Or presents alternatives."]],"related":["129","169","202","216"]},"094":{"intro":"The passive voice puts the action or its receiver in focus. A basic passive is formed with be + past participle. It is especially useful when the doer is unknown, unimportant or obvious.","sections":[{"title":"Active and passive: one event, different focus","explain":"In an active sentence, the subject performs the action. In a passive sentence, the object of a corresponding active clause often becomes the subject.","points":["Active: A technician repairs the machine. Passive: The machine is repaired by a technician.","Passive form: subject + am/is/are + past participle (present simple).","The past participle may be regular (-ed) or irregular: written, made, built, given.","Use by + person/agent only when naming the doer adds useful information.","Not every verb can be made passive: an intransitive verb such as arrive has no direct object."],"examples":[["English is spoken in many countries.","Focus on the language, not individual speakers."],["The rooms are cleaned daily.","Plural subject → are cleaned."],["The report is written by the secretary.","By phrase identifies the agent."]]},{"title":"Negatives and questions","explain":"Passive verbs still follow the normal question and negative patterns for the verb be.","points":["Negative: The package is not delivered on Sundays.","Question: Is the package delivered on Sundays?","Plural: Are the products made locally?","The passive does not mean the same as a continuous action unless a continuous form is used."],"examples":[["Is the equipment checked every day?","Question formed by moving is to the front."],["These forms are not accepted online.","Be + not + participle."]]}],"mistakes":[["The files are send today.","The files are sent today.","Passive requires past participle sent."],["The letters is delivered.","The letters are delivered.","Plural subject needs are."],["The accident happened by a driver.","The accident happened.","Happen is intransitive and does not form an ordinary passive."]],"quiz":[["Complete: The meals ____ prepared daily.",["is","are","be"],1,"Plural meals takes are."],["A passive verb uses:",["be + past participle","be + -ing only","have + infinitive"],0,"Be + past participle is the standard form."],["Choose the passive.",["Someone checks the door.","The door is checked.","The door checks."],1,"The door receives the action."]],"related":["118","119","153","200"]},"009":{"intro":"Nouns name people, places, things, groups, substances, events and ideas. Knowing the noun type helps you choose articles, plurals, quantifiers and verb agreement.","sections":[{"title":"Noun families and jobs","explain":"A noun may be concrete (desk, hospital) or abstract (honesty, advice). Proper nouns identify particular people or places and normally use capital letters.","points":["Common: nurse, city, idea. Proper: Sameera, Doha, London.","Countable: a form / two forms. Uncountable: advice, equipment, information.","Collective: team, family, staff. Abstract: courage, beauty, hope.","A noun can be the subject, object or complement of a clause."],"examples":[["The nurse checked the equipment.","Nurse is subject; equipment is object."],["Honesty matters at work.","An abstract noun is the subject."],["My family lives abroad.","Family is a collective noun."]]},{"title":"Articles and noun phrases","explain":"A noun phrase may contain a determiner, descriptive adjectives and a head noun. Singular countable nouns usually need a determiner.","points":["a helpful colleague; the large hospital; my blue notebook.","Avoid 'I bought book'—say I bought a book / the book / my book.","A/an depends on the following sound, not just the letter: an hour, a university.","A noun may modify another noun: hospital ward, English lesson."],"examples":[["She works in a busy hospital.","Determiner + adjective + noun."],["I need information about the course.","Information is uncountable."]]}],"mistakes":[["She gave me an advice.","She gave me some advice.","Advice is uncountable."],["I bought pen.","I bought a pen.","A singular count noun usually needs a determiner."],["The London is beautiful.","London is beautiful.","Many city names take no article."]],"quiz":[["Which is uncountable?",["equipment","desk","appointment"],0,"Equipment has no ordinary plural."],["Choose a proper noun.",["country","London","hospital"],1,"London names one particular city."],["Choose the correct noun phrase.",["helpful a nurse","a helpful nurse","a nurse helpful"],1,"Determiner usually precedes adjective + noun."]],"related":["025","026","027","218"]},"025":{"intro":"Not all plurals add -s. English nouns include irregular changes, unchanged plurals, foreign plurals and words that are plural in form but singular in meaning.","sections":[{"title":"Common irregular plurals","explain":"Learn the irregular form together with each noun. Do not apply the regular -s rule when the noun has a fixed irregular plural.","points":["man → men; woman → women; child → children; person → people.","foot → feet; tooth → teeth; mouse → mice; goose → geese.","sheep → sheep; deer → deer; fish → fish (fishes can refer to species).","analysis → analyses; crisis → crises; criterion → criteria.","Some Latin/Greek plurals vary by register: index → indexes/indices; formula → formulas/formulae."],"examples":[["Two children were waiting outside.","Children is already plural."],["The analyses were reviewed.","Analysis → analyses."],["Several sheep were grazing.","No change in plural form."]]},{"title":"Spelling and agreement","explain":"Some irregular-looking endings are regular in modern English; avoid inventing universal rules.","points":["knife → knives; life → lives, but roof → roofs and chief → chiefs.","city → cities (consonant + y), but day → days (vowel + y).","photo → photos; piano → pianos; tomato → tomatoes.","Some mass nouns remain uncountable: furniture, advice, information.","News ends in -s but usually takes singular agreement: The news is good."],"examples":[["Those boxes contain old photographs.","Photograph → photographs."],["The news is encouraging.","News is normally grammatically singular."]]}],"mistakes":[["childs","children","Irregular plural."],["informations","information","Uncountable in the usual meaning."],["The news are interesting.","The news is interesting.","News usually takes a singular verb."]],"quiz":[["Plural of analysis:",["analysises","analyses","analysi"],1,"Analysis → analyses."],["Plural of sheep:",["sheep","sheeps","sheepen"],0,"No change."],["Choose the correct sentence.",["The news are good.","The news is good.","The news have good."],1,"News generally takes singular agreement."]],"related":["009","026","218"]},"026":{"intro":"Countable nouns can be counted as separate units. Uncountable nouns normally refer to substances, masses or ideas that need a quantity expression.","sections":[{"title":"Articles and quantifiers","explain":"The kind of noun determines whether you can use a/an, a plural form or much/many. Some words are countable in one meaning and uncountable in another.","points":["a chair / chairs; one ticket / two tickets.","water, rice, furniture, luggage, advice, information and equipment are usually uncountable.","many chairs; much water; a few chairs; a little water.","some information; a piece of advice; two pieces of furniture.","Coffee can be uncountable as a drink, but two coffees means two servings in a café."],"examples":[["There are many chairs but little space.","Many with count; little with mass."],["Could you give me some advice?","Advice is uncountable."],["We ordered two coffees.","Countable servings."]]},{"title":"More meaning-sensitive nouns","explain":"Some nouns change their grammar with meaning. This is common in professional and everyday English.","points":["experience (knowledge) is often uncountable; an experience (event) is countable.","work (activity) is uncountable; a work of art is countable.","paper (material) is uncountable; a paper (article) is countable.","hair (collective) is often uncountable; a hair (one strand) is countable."],"examples":[["She has extensive nursing experience.","Mass meaning."],["It was an unforgettable experience.","One event."]]}],"mistakes":[["many informations","a lot of information","Information is usually uncountable."],["three furnitures","three pieces of furniture","Use a counting unit."],["a rice","some rice / a bowl of rice","Rice is normally uncountable."]],"quiz":[["Which fits 'advice'?",["an advice","some advice","three advices"],1,"Advice is mass/uncountable."],["Countable form for luggage:",["two luggages","two pieces of luggage","many luggage"],1,"Piece is a counting unit."],["Choose a countable use:",["a memorable experience","much an experience","an information"],0,"One event is an experience."]],"related":["009","025","027","121"]},"027":{"intro":"Articles signal how a noun is introduced or identified. A/an presents one non-specific singular count item; the refers to something identifiable in context; zero article is also meaningful.","sections":[{"title":"A/an and the","explain":"Use a/an with a singular count noun when the listener does not yet know which one. The can refer to something already mentioned, unique in context or made specific by a phrase.","points":["I saw a dog. The dog was friendly.","a before consonant sounds; an before vowel sounds: an hour, a university.","the manager of our branch (the phrase identifies one).","A singular countable noun usually needs a determiner: a plan / the plan / this plan / my plan."],"examples":[["She opened a window. The window faced the garden.","First mention then identifiable."],["He is an honest man.","Honest starts with a vowel sound."],["This is a useful idea.","Useful starts with a /j/ consonant sound."]]},{"title":"Zero article","explain":"General plural and mass nouns often appear without an article. Names, languages and meals also often have zero article, with exceptions.","points":["Doctors need clear communication (general plural).","Water is essential (general mass noun).","She speaks English (language).","We had breakfast at seven (meal).","The water in this bottle tastes unusual (specific water)."],"examples":[["Books are useful for study.","General plural."],["The books on that shelf are mine.","Specific set."]]}],"mistakes":[["an university","a university","/juː/ starts with a consonant sound."],["I bought the new phone (first unidentified mention).","I bought a new phone.","Use a when first introducing one non-specific item."],["The honesty is important (general idea).","Honesty is important.","General abstract noun often has zero article."]],"quiz":[["Choose the article: ____ hour",["a","an","the only"],1,"Hour begins with a vowel sound."],["Choose a general statement.",["The cats are mammals (specific cats).","Cats are mammals.","A cats are mammals."],1,"General plural often has no article."],["Complete: I saw a film. ____ film was excellent.",["The","A","An"],0,"The film is now identifiable."]],"related":["009","026","067","123","161","219"]},"034":{"intro":"Present simple describes regular habits, stable facts, preferences and schedules. Use the base verb with I, you, we and they.","sections":[{"title":"Positive, negative and question patterns","explain":"I/you/we/they + base verb; use do not/don't + base verb for negatives; do + subject + base verb for questions. The verb be follows different patterns.","points":["I work; you study; we live; they travel.","I do not work on Sundays; they don't live here.","Do you speak English? Do they work here?","Be is different: I am, you/we/they are; Are you ready? not Do you are ready?","Frequency adverbs usually come before the lexical verb: We often practise."],"examples":[["We study English every day.","Regular routine."],["They do not open on Fridays.","Negative uses do not."],["Do you work nearby?","Question uses do."]]},{"title":"Uses beyond habits","explain":"Present simple also describes general truths, instructions, narrative summaries and timetables, including events in the future.","points":["Water freezes at 0°C under standard conditions.","The train leaves at 7:15 tomorrow (fixed timetable).","First, you turn on the device (instruction or demonstration).","I love this book (state).","Do not use present simple for an action happening right now when present continuous is needed."],"examples":[["The shop opens at nine.","A regular timetable."],["I know the answer.","Know is a state verb in its usual meaning."]]}],"mistakes":[["Do you works here?","Do you work here?","After do, use base form."],["We not live here.","We do not live here.","Negatives need auxiliary do."],["I am work in a hospital.","I work in a hospital.","Do not add am to present simple main verbs."]],"quiz":[["Choose the negative.",["They not work.","They don't work.","They doesn't work."],1,"They + do not."],["Choose a question.",["Do you study?","Are you study?","Do you studies?"],0,"Do + subject + base verb."],["'The train leaves at six' describes:",["a fixed timetable","only a completed past event","a reported question"],0,"Present simple often describes scheduled departures."]],"related":["035","036","037","039","045"]},"035":{"intro":"With he, she and it, present simple positive verbs often take -s or -es. The third-person rule applies to almost all lexical verbs, with a few special forms.","sections":[{"title":"Spelling of the third-person -s","explain":"The ending is chosen by spelling and pronunciation. It does not appear after the auxiliary does in negative statements or questions.","points":["work → works; read → reads; sleep → sleeps.","watch → watches; go → goes; do → does; fix → fixes.","study → studies (consonant + y), but play → plays (vowel + y).","have → has; be → is.","Does she work? She doesn't work. (base verb after does)."],"examples":[["He watches the news every evening.","Watch + es."],["She studies English online.","Study → studies."],["It rains a lot in winter.","Rain + s."]]},{"title":"Pronunciation and agreement","explain":"The -s ending is pronounced /s/, /z/ or /ɪz/ depending on the preceding sound. Subject agreement matters even when other words come between subject and verb.","points":["works /s/; lives /z/; watches /ɪz/.","My colleague, who works nights, lives nearby.","Each student needs a book.","The subject controls the main verb, not a nearby plural word.","Modal verbs are different: He can work, not he cans work."],"examples":[["The manager of these offices works here.","Manager is singular."],["She can speak French.","After modal can use base verb."]]}],"mistakes":[["He go to work.","He goes to work.","Third person positive ends in -es here."],["She doesn't works.","She doesn't work.","Does already carries the agreement."],["He can speaks.","He can speak.","Modal + bare verb."]],"quiz":[["Complete: She ____ English.",["study","studies","studys"],1,"Consonant + y changes to -ies."],["Choose the correct question.",["Does he works?","Does he work?","Do he work?"],1,"Does + base verb."],["Correct modal pattern:",["He must goes.","He must go.","He musts go."],1,"Modals do not take third-person -s."]],"related":["034","037","218"]},"089":{"intro":"Some English verbs take another verb ending in -ing; others take to + infinitive. A few can take both, sometimes with different meaning.","sections":[{"title":"Frequent verb patterns","explain":"Learn the first verb and the next form as a unit rather than guessing from your first language.","points":["enjoy, avoid, finish, suggest, mind + -ing.","want, decide, plan, hope, agree, need + to + verb.","like, love, hate, prefer can take -ing or to + verb; meaning may vary in context.","After a preposition, use -ing: good at speaking, before leaving.","Let and make (active) take an object + bare infinitive: let me go; made us wait."],"examples":[["I enjoy learning new words.","Enjoy + -ing."],["We decided to leave early.","Decide + to-infinitive."],["She is interested in improving her writing.","In + -ing."]]},{"title":"Meaning can change","explain":"Stop, remember, forget and try are especially useful to examine because the second verb form changes the relationship between actions.","points":["stop smoking = end the activity; stop to smoke = pause another activity for a smoke.","remember locking the door = recall the earlier action; remember to lock it = don't forget the task.","try doing = experiment with a method; try to do = make an effort.","Avoid 'enjoy to read' in standard usage."],"examples":[["He stopped talking when the teacher arrived.","The talking ended."],["He stopped to talk to the teacher.","He paused another activity to talk."]]}],"mistakes":[["enjoy to learn","enjoy learning","Enjoy takes -ing."],["decided leaving","decided to leave","Decide takes to-infinitive."],["look forward to see you","look forward to seeing you","Here to is a preposition."]],"quiz":[["Complete: I enjoy ____.",["read","reading","to read"],1,"Enjoy + -ing."],["Complete: We decided ____.",["leave","leaving","to leave"],2,"Decide + infinitive."],["'Stopped to rest' means:",["resting ended","paused something else to rest","remembered resting"],1,"Infinitive expresses purpose."]],"related":["124","166","198"]},"090":{"intro":"Relative clauses add information about a person or thing without starting a completely new sentence. Who, which and that are common relative words.","sections":[{"title":"Who, which and that","explain":"A defining relative clause identifies which person or thing we mean. Choose the relative pronoun according to its role.","points":["who for people: the nurse who helped me.","which for things: the book which I borrowed.","that for people/things in many defining clauses: the film that we watched.","A defining clause is not usually separated by commas.","When the relative word is an object, it can often be omitted: the film (that) we watched."],"examples":[["The teacher who helped us is here.","Who is the subject of helped."],["This is the book that I bought.","That is the object of bought."],["The book I bought was useful.","Object relative omitted."]]},{"title":"Subject versus object relatives","explain":"You may omit an object relative pronoun, but normally not a subject relative pronoun in a finite defining relative clause.","points":["The worker who arrived early is prepared (who = subject, required).","The worker who I met yesterday is prepared (who = object, can be omitted).","Don't repeat an object pronoun after the relative word: the book that I read, not the book that I read it."],"examples":[["The woman I met yesterday is a doctor.","Whom/who as object is omitted."],["The laptop that broke was old.","That is a subject and remains."]]}],"mistakes":[["the man who he works here","the man who works here","Don't duplicate the relative subject."],["the book that I read it","the book that I read","Don't repeat the object."],["the worker arrived early is ready","the worker who arrived early is ready","A subject relative word is needed."]],"quiz":[["Choose the subject relative.",["the person who called","the person called me yesterday is","the person who she called"],0,"Who is subject of called."],["Object relative can often:",["be omitted","always be omitted as subject","take a repeated object pronoun"],0,"Object relative pronoun omission is possible."],["Defining clauses generally:",["lack separating commas","always require commas","are always parenthetical"],0,"They identify the noun."]],"related":["113","114","130","159"]},"086":{"intro":"The first conditional describes a real or possible future situation and its likely result. The condition is generally in the present form; the result may use will, can, may or an imperative.","sections":[{"title":"Structure and meaning","explain":"A common structure is if + present simple, will + base verb. The main clause can come first or second.","points":["If it rains, we will stay inside.","We will stay inside if it rains.","Use a comma after an introductory if-clause; usually no comma is needed when the if-clause comes last.","May/can/might and imperatives are possible in the result: If you finish early, you can leave.","Do not normally put will in the ordinary if-clause when stating a condition."],"examples":[["If she studies, she will improve.","Real future possibility."],["We can begin if everyone is ready.","Can in main clause."],["If you need help, call reception.","Imperative main clause."]]},{"title":"If versus when and unless","explain":"If signals uncertainty; when expects the event to occur. Unless introduces an exception. The verb after these time/condition words is normally present for future reference.","points":["If the courier arrives, call me (arrival uncertain).","When the courier arrives, call me (arrival expected).","Unless it rains, we'll walk (except if it rains).","As soon as he finishes, we will start (future time clause uses present simple)."],"examples":[["When the train arrives, we will board.","Expected event."],["Unless the road is closed, we will drive.","An exception condition."]]}],"mistakes":[["If it will rain, we will stay.","If it rains, we will stay.","Use present simple for ordinary future condition."],["When she will arrive, call me.","When she arrives, call me.","Future time clause takes present simple."],["If he studies, he would pass (ordinary likely future).","If he studies, he will pass.","Will suits a likely real future outcome."]],"quiz":[["Complete: If it ____, we'll wait.",["rains","will rain","raining"],0,"Present simple in ordinary if-clause."],["'When you arrive' implies:",["arrival expected","arrival impossible","past regret"],0,"When treats arrival as expected."],["Choose the first conditional.",["If I had known, I would have helped.","If I know, I will help.","If I were rich, I would travel."],1,"Present + will."]],"related":["088","109","111","148"]},"088":{"intro":"The zero conditional describes general facts, repeated consequences and standard procedures: if/when + present simple, present simple.","sections":[{"title":"Facts, habits and instructions","explain":"Both clauses typically describe what regularly happens, not a single uncertain future outcome.","points":["If you heat ice, it melts.","When a learner practises regularly, confidence improves.","If the alarm sounds, the doors close (procedure, if true in that system).","When may replace if when an event is reliably expected or repeated.","Scientific examples depend on conditions: water boils at approximately 100°C at sea-level pressure."],"examples":[["If you mix red and blue paint, you get purple.","General result in basic paint mixing."],["When I miss breakfast, I get hungry early.","Repeated personal pattern."],["If the temperature falls below zero, water can freeze.","General possibility."]]},{"title":"Zero versus first conditional","explain":"The zero conditional refers to generally true outcomes. The first conditional refers to one real future possibility.","points":["If I skip lunch, I feel hungry = repeated pattern.","If I skip lunch today, I will feel hungry = specific future expectation.","Use appropriate tense based on what the speaker actually means."],"examples":[["If you press this button, the screen lights up.","Regular machine behaviour."],["If you press it tomorrow, the screen will light up.","A specific future event."]]}],"mistakes":[["If you heated ice, it melts (general rule).","If you heat ice, it melts.","Present + present for general truth."],["If water boils, it will always evaporated.","When water boils, it evaporates.","Use the suitable verb and present simple for a general rule."],["If I will be tired, I sleep.","When I am tired, I sleep.","Habitual condition needs present simple."]],"quiz":[["Zero conditional often uses:",["present + present","past perfect + would have","will + will"],0,"General result."],["'If I'm tired, I sleep early' refers to:",["a routine","a past regret","a command about yesterday"],0,"Repeated behaviour."],["Choose a first conditional instead:",["If it snows tomorrow, we will cancel.","If ice melts, it becomes water.","When I run, I sweat."],0,"Specific future possibility."]],"related":["086","109","111"]},"067":{"intro":"A/an, the and zero article change the meaning of noun phrases. Beyond first mention, you need to decide whether a listener can identify the reference.","sections":[{"title":"General versus specific","explain":"A/an introduces one unspecified countable example. The points to a specific identifiable reference. Zero article often expresses a general category or mass.","points":["I need a pen (any one); I need the pen on the desk (specific).","Doctors work long hours (general plural); The doctors on this ward are busy (specified).","Education matters (general abstract noun); The education he received was excellent (specific kind).","A singular count noun normally needs a determiner."],"examples":[["A meeting was arranged. The meeting begins at ten.","First mention then known."],["We drink water, but the water here tastes different.","Generic versus specific mass noun."],["She has a university degree.","University starts with a consonant /j/ sound."]]},{"title":"Fixed common expressions","explain":"Articles are often conventional in expressions; do not decide by translation alone.","points":["go to work, at home, by bus, have breakfast (often zero article).","go to the cinema, play the piano (traditional/common British patterns).","the United Kingdom, the Netherlands, the Thames; but Sri Lanka, Qatar, London.","in hospital (British patient context); in the hospital can identify a building or be used in other varieties."],"examples":[["She goes to work by train.","No article in this transport expression."],["They visited the United Kingdom last year.","The appears in the country name."]]}],"mistakes":[["I go to the work every day.","I go to work every day.","Work as a destination normally has zero article."],["She is in a hospital (meaning ordinary patient stay in BrE).","She is in hospital.","In hospital is a common British patient expression."],["The Qatar is hot.","Qatar is hot.","Most country names have no article."]],"quiz":[["Choose the general plural.",["The students are curious (particular group).","Students are curious.","A students are curious."],1,"Zero article for general plural."],["Choose British patient use.",["in hospital","in the hospital always","at a hospital only"],0,"In hospital is common for being a patient."],["Country with 'the':",["the Qatar","the Sri Lanka","the United Kingdom"],2,"Part of official name."]],"related":["027","123","161","219"]},"073":{"intro":"Present perfect connects a past action or experience with the present. The basic form is have/has + past participle.","sections":[{"title":"Life experiences","explain":"Use present perfect when the exact past time is not the point. The speaker's experience up to now matters.","points":["I have visited London.","She has never tried sushi.","Have you ever worked abroad?","Present perfect usually does not combine with a finished past-time marker like yesterday or in 2020.","Gone and been can have different meanings: She's gone to the shop (not back); she's been to the shop (visited and returned)."],"examples":[["I have never travelled to Japan.","Life experience up to now."],["Has he ever seen snow?","Ever in an experience question."],["They have finished their training.","Current completion/relevance."]]},{"title":"Result now and unfinished time","explain":"Present perfect may express a current result or something continuing through a period that includes now.","points":["I've lost my keys (I cannot find them now).","She has worked here for three years (and still works here).","This week, we have had two meetings (week ongoing).","Use have with I/you/we/they; has with he/she/it.","Past participles: seen, gone, done, written, taken."],"examples":[["We have known each other since 2019.","State continues to now."],["I've already sent the email.","Current completed result."]]}],"mistakes":[["I have visited London yesterday.","I visited London yesterday.","Finished past-time marker takes past simple."],["She has went home.","She has gone home.","Gone is the participle."],["He have finished.","He has finished.","Third-person singular takes has."]],"quiz":[["Present perfect structure:",["have/has + participle","had + infinitive","be + base verb"],0,"Have/has + participle."],["Choose an experience question.",["Have you ever visited Bath?","Did you ever visited Bath?","Are you ever visit Bath?"],0,"Have you ever + participle."],["With 'yesterday' ordinarily use:",["present perfect","past simple","future perfect"],1,"Finished past time."]],"related":["074","075","097","137"]},"075":{"intro":"Present perfect focuses on present relevance or unfinished periods; past simple places an action in a finished past time.","sections":[{"title":"The key time distinction","explain":"Look at the time expression and what the speaker wants to emphasise—not only whether the action is complete.","points":["I visited London in 2022 (finished time).","I have visited London twice (experience up to now).","She worked here for two years (typically no longer works here); She has worked here for two years (still works here).","Have you finished? (current result) versus Did you finish yesterday? (finished past time).","Just/already/yet frequently occur with present perfect in British usage."],"examples":[["I saw him yesterday.","Definite finished past time."],["I have seen him this week.","Week may still be ongoing."],["She has lived here since 2020.","Continues to now."]]},{"title":"Questions and negatives","explain":"Form present perfect questions with have/has, not did. Past simple uses did for lexical verbs.","points":["Have you eaten? / Yes, I have.","Did you eat at noon? / Yes, I did.","I haven't eaten yet (relevant now).","I didn't eat at noon (past time).","The present perfect cannot normally appear with 'last Tuesday' as its event-time adverbial."],"examples":[["Have they completed the task yet?","Current result."],["Did they complete it on Monday?","Specific finished past day."]]}],"mistakes":[["I have met him last week.","I met him last week.","Past simple with finished past time."],["Did you ever been there?","Have you ever been there?","Ever + life experience often uses present perfect."],["I am here since 2022.","I have been here since 2022.","Continuing state up to now."]],"quiz":[["With 'last month' choose:",["present perfect","past simple","future perfect"],1,"Finished time."],["Choose a continuing state.",["I have worked here since April.","I worked here next April.","I work here yesterday."],0,"Since marks an ongoing period."],["Choose the correct question.",["Have you eaten yet?","Did you eaten yet?","Are you eaten yet?"],0,"Have + participle."]],"related":["073","074","097","137"]},"097":{"intro":"Present perfect simple and continuous both link past and present. The simple often highlights a result or total; the continuous often highlights the activity or duration.","sections":[{"title":"Result versus activity","explain":"Use have/has + participle for perfect simple; have/has been + -ing for perfect continuous.","points":["I have written three emails (completed quantity).","I have been writing emails all morning (activity and duration).","She has painted the room (perhaps complete).","She has been painting the room (focus on ongoing/recent activity).","State verbs such as know often use simple: I have known her for years."],"examples":[["I've finished two chapters.","Completed amount."],["I've been studying for two hours.","Activity extending through a period."],["It has been raining.","Evidence or ongoing/recent rain."]]},{"title":"For, since and temporary effects","explain":"Both forms can occur with for/since depending on meaning. Do not teach the distinction as an absolute rule.","points":["I have lived here for five years / I have been living here for five years (both possible; different focus).","I've been running, so I'm tired (visible consequence).","She has read the book (finished result).","I've known him since 2010 (state; continuous usually unnatural)."],"examples":[["She has worked here since May.","Continuing state/employment."],["She has been working on this problem since May.","Emphasis on an ongoing project."]]}],"mistakes":[["I have been knowing him.","I have known him.","Know usually takes simple perfect."],["I have been written three letters.","I have written three letters.","Perfect simple for counted finished results."],["I has been working.","I have been working.","I takes have."]],"quiz":[["Completed quantity:",["I've written five pages.","I've been writing five pages (necessarily complete).","I am wrote five pages."],0,"Simple highlights a total."],["Ongoing activity:",["I've been studying.","I have study.","I has studied."],0,"Have been + -ing."],["State verb:",["I've known her for years.","I've been knowing her for years.","I have know her."],0,"Know usually takes simple."]],"related":["073","075","137"]}};
